{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KnwCAZwAM7mJsZrC7Z13ea","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tSOURCE: Animal Crossing: City Folk\n2\t\n3\tGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n4\t\n5\tPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n6\t\n7\tGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n8\t\n9\tResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n10\t\n11\tPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-171824].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n12\t\n13\tTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n14\t\n15\tArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n16\t\n17\tAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n18\t\n19\tAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n20\t\n21\tOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n22\t\n23\tWorking Environment and deliverable:\n24\tCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-171824] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-171824].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n25\t\n26\tPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n27\t\n28\tTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n29\t\n30\tEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n31\t\n32\t## This computer\n33\tChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n34\t\n35\t- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n36\t- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n37\t- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n38\t- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n39\t  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n40\t  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n41\t  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n42\t- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n43\t- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n44\t- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n45\t- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n46\t- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n47\t\n48\t### What this computer doesn't have\n49\t\n50\tNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n51\t\n52\t- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n53\t- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n54\t- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n55\t- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n56\t- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n57\t- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n58\t\n59\t### Sharing this computer\n60\t\n61\t- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n62\t- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n63\t- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n64\t- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n65\t\n66\t## Goal\n67\t\n68\tBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n69\t\n70\tScope\n71\t\n72\tCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n73\t\n74\tInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n75\t\n76\tAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n77\t\n78\tMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n79\t\n80\tNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n81\t\n82\t## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n83\tDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n84\t\n85\tDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n86\t\n87\tTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n88\t\n89\t## Done\n90\tNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n91\t\n92\t## Source Information\n93\tCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n94\t- Date: 2026-11-05 17:18:24\n95\t- Source: the work it's based on\n96\t- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n97\t- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n98\t- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n99\t- Art Direction: style used\n100\t- Animation: system used\n101\t- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n102\t- Online: network features used, or none\n103\t- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n104\t- Research: research done, or none\n105\t- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n106\t- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n107\t- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n108\t\n109\tExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n110\t{\n111\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n112\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n113\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n114\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n115\t  \"aiHub\": {\n116\t    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n117\t    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n118\t    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n119\t    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n120\t    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n121\t    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n122\t    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n123\t    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n124\t    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n125\t    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n126\t  }\n127\t}\n128\tThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n129\t\n130\tTesting Feedback\n131\t\n132\t- How many issues did testing catch?\n133\t- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n134\t- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n135\t- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n136\t\n137\tEngine Feedback\n138\t\n139\t- What did you like most about the engine?\n140\t- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n141\t- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n142\t- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n143\t- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n144\t- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n145\t\n146\tPrompt Feedback\n147\t\n148\t- What did you like most about this prompt?\n149\t- What was confusing?\n150\t- What was the hardest thing to do?\n151\t- What would you change in this prompt?\n152\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7fc0c99-6050-4a36-bbad-13de512b63e9","uuid":"f76f382a-2c11-464f-887a-ef1a9a3c2821","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:18:27.622Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/TASK.md","content":"SOURCE: Animal Crossing: City Folk\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-171824].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-171824] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-171824].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-05 17:18:24\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","numLines":152,"startLine":1,"totalLines":152}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KnwCAZwAM7mJsZrC7Z13ea","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkiQfNFfSjhCqV7uCnt4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019drUbExmgropzoQXR5QEZ3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/PLAN.md","limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4666,"cache_read_input_tokens":14699,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4666},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7fc0c99-6050-4a36-bbad-13de512b63e9","uuid":"c7ba76d8-a917-4fbb-8bdc-af21fc1dce1c","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:18:28.123Z","request_id":"req_011CgkiQf9cPvAVhFduNLyzJ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_019drUbExmgropzoQXR5QEZ3":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019drUbExmgropzoQXR5QEZ3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Game Plan: a village life sim, one bus ride from the Big City\n2\t\n3\tSource: *Animal Crossing: City Folk*. You build an **original** game inspired by it, from this plan and your TASK.md, without its author. Read TASK.md first, then this whole file once.\n4\t\n5\t**Contents:** 0 Using this plan · 1 The game · 2 Engine and packaging (verified recipes) · 3 Time, calendar, weather · 4 The village · 5 You: avatar, movement, tools · 6 Things to do · 7 Money · 8 Home · 9 Neighbours · 10 Town services · 11 The City · 12 The year · 13 Day One · 14 Writing bible · 15 UI and UX · 16 Art direction · 17 Animation · 18 Audio · 19 Multiplayer · 20 Title, attract, pause, settings, dev mode, About, saves · 21 Code architecture · 22 Testing · 23 Build order and cut rules · 24 Finish checklist and TASK.md coverage\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## 0. Using this plan\n10\t\n11\t- **Title.** It is yours to choose, the way TASK.md says: brainstorm silently, pick with a real random number generator, do it first. This plan says \"the game\" throughout. The riff must stand on its own joke (see *Originality* in section 1).\n12\t- **Names and paths.** TASK.md names your deliverable HTML, your dev folder and your exit interview file. This plan never does. Every path below is relative to your dev folder.\n13\t- **[verified]** means the author ran it on engine kit 0.46.0, on this machine, before writing this. Treat it as known good. If your kit is newer and something differs, read the kit's `CHANGELOG.md`.\n14\t- **Numbers are defaults.** They live in one tuning table (section 20). Change them when play shows they're wrong.\n15\t- **Content counts are written floor → target.** Every system ships. Under pressure, content may drop to its floor; a system never ships half-built (section 23).\n16\t- **Changing a decision:** you may, if it fails in practice. Keep the pillar it served.\n17\t\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## 1. The game\n21\t\n22\t**Pitch.** You step off the last bus at the end of the line and, within ninety seconds, owe a goose 8,000 beans. The village is tiny, its neighbours are odd and opinionated, and every day has something to catch, dig, grow, sell, overhear or regret. One bus ride away, the Big City glows: salons, auctions, couture, a theatre, and a magpie selling art that is \"only forty percent stolen\". The village keeps time whether you're there or not, and it will mention it.\n23\t\n24\t| Field | Decision |\n25\t|---|---|\n26\t| **Genre** | Life sim / social sim (AI Hub genre: `Life Sim`) |\n27\t| **Perspective** | Isometric 3/4 orthographic diorama: engine `iso` preset, yaw 45°, pitch 35°, zoom 1.6, with 90° camera turns. Same camera indoors, as a dollhouse cutaway. Chosen from side-by-side renders: straight-on (yaw 0) reads flat and roofs turn into colour blocks; 45° reads like a tabletop model and suits the engine's pixel pipeline. |\n28\t| **Length** | A 16-day calendar year, about 2.5 to 5 hours to live fully; most features are reachable in the first hour; a player can sleep ahead to any season without dev mode. Play continues after the ending. |\n29\t| **Players** | 1 solo; 1–4 in AI Hub sessions; 1–4 visiting on its own (section 19) |\n30\t\n31\t**What makes the source distinctive, and our version of it:**\n32\t\n33\t| The source's essence | Our version |\n34\t|---|---|\n35\t| The world runs on a clock and calendar, with or without you | Village Time or Real Clock, a different tune every hour, weeds and letters that pile up, neighbours who noticed you left |\n36\t| A small town of animal neighbours with personality and gossip | Eight odd misfits with tics, routines, feuds and a memory of what you did |\n37\t| Cheerful debt to a shopkeeper who owns everything | Mrs. Gander, a terrifyingly courteous goose and the village's bean counter |\n38\t| Collect everything, donate to a museum | Fish, bugs, fossils and art, one pun at a time, for a near-sighted curator |\n39\t| City Folk's signature: a bus to a city of shops | The Big City: bus ballads, neon nights, salon, couture fashion check, auction, theatre, shady art dealer, home-rating bureau, fortunes, café, crowds |\n40\t| Visiting friends' towns | AI Hub round, and Visit mode on its own |\n41\t| Warm, deadpan writing and babbling voices | Writing bible (section 14), code-made gibberish voices |\n42\t| Seasonal holidays | Four festivals, three contests, concerts, snow |\n43\t| Self-expression | Pixel patterns, a village chime you compose, furniture sets, outfits, hair |\n44\t\n45\t**Pillars**\n46\t\n47\t1. **The village has a pulse.** It keeps time, remembers and carries on.\n48\t2. **Neighbours are the game.** They should be the thing a player tells a friend about.\n49\t3. **Small verbs, big satisfaction.** Shake, swing, cast, dig. Each has anticipation, impact, a sound, a sparkle and usually a joke.\n50\t4. **Two worlds, one bus.** Home is slow and soft. The City is loud and lit. The ride between them is a threshold you feel.\n51\t5. **Nothing is urgent.** No failure, no combat, no game over, no stamina. The debt is a running joke. In Village Time the clock stops while you talk or browse.\n52\t6. **Ten feet away.** Big type, few choices on screen, everything within two presses.\n53\t\n54\t**Not in the game:** combat, stamina, terraforming, touch controls, voice or text chat, couch split-screen.\n55\t\n56\t**Originality (hard rules).** TASK.md asks for an original game inspired by the source. Genre and mechanics are fair game. Its names, characters, art, music, maps and look are not.\n57\t\n58\t- No character, name or term from the series: no Nook, Bells, Gyroids, K.K., Resetti, Happy Home Academy, Able Sisters, Stalk Market or turnips.\n59\t- No species-for-role copies: no raccoon or tanuki shopkeeper, owl curator, mole scold, dog musician, hedgehog tailors, kappa driver, shih tzu clerk, fox art dealer, poodle stylist, giraffe couturier.\n60\t- Neighbours are species the series' villager roster doesn't use (wombat, quokka, moose, platypus…), which also gives the game its own silhouette.\n61\t- No melody from the series, no imitation of a recognisable tune. All music is newly composed.\n62\t- No copying of its character designs or UI layouts. No rolling-log curved horizon.\n63\t- The title is a pun that works by itself, not the source's name with a letter changed; its logo never carries the source's name.\n64\t- The source is named only where TASK.md requires it: About/Credits and the catalogue fields.\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## 2. Engine and packaging: verified recipes\n69\t\n70\tThe kit is a WebGPU engine that renders chunky low-poly 3D as pixel art at 480×270. Read the kit's `GUIDE.md` whole and `ASSETS.md` whole; search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`. What follows is what those docs don't tell you for *this* game.\n71\t\n72\t### What was tested\n73\t\n74\t| Question | Result [verified] |\n75\t|---|---|\n76\t| **Browser flags for WebGPU here** | **The engine needs `--use-angle=swiftshader`.** With only `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`, as TASK.md lists them, it never renders: the device is lost (`Instance dropped in popErrorScope`), `gpuRecoveries` climbs, the frame count sticks, the picture stays blank. Use the kit's own `check.mjs` set: `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`. |\n77\t| One HTML on `file://`, WebGPU on | Works. Boots in 1–2 s on the software GPU. Inline modules avoid the `file://` module ban the guide warns about. |\n78\t| Engine byte-identical inside the page | Works, including inside Hub harness iframes: recipe below |\n79\t| Packs in one file | `loadPack('packs/<id>.json')` works from `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__` data URLs |\n80\t| Custom character rig | `buildModel` pivots + `compileClips` + `THREE.AnimationMixer` work. `crossFadeTo` blends. `Animator`, `PoseLayers` and `restPoseOf` are **not** exported by the kit; `validateClip`, `renderSheet`, `gaitClip`, `SpringChain`, `Squash` are. |\n81\t| `Engine.headless` in Node | Runs a scene with water, grass, weather, packs and custom rigs. 3,600 steps took 54 ms on a small scene. `hud` works there with `canvas: null`. |\n82\t| Mixed-case text via `hud.sprite` | Legible at 1080p. 3,000 glyphs a frame changing every frame showed no measurable slowdown. (`hud.text` is uppercase only.) |\n83\t| `engine.paused = true` | **The game's `update` stops, so a menu drawn there dies.** `timeScale = 0` keeps `update` running with `dt = 0`. See the pause rule below. |\n84\t| Rotating or zooming the iso camera live | `engine.setCamera({...}, { syncUrl: false })` every frame is cheap (31 calls: 3 ms) |\n85\t| Wheel for tools instead of zoom | `minZoom === maxZoom` locks the engine's wheel and +/− zoom; `input.wheel` still reads in `update` |\n86\t| Mouse | `input.pointer` tracks hover with no button held; `camera.rayAt` + a ground plane gives the tile |\n87\t| Stub DualSense | `input.gamepadButtons` with custom codes works; `isDown('PadCross')` true, `input.snapshot()` shows it |\n88\t| See-through occluders | Swapping a mesh to a shared `toonMaterial(c, { dissolve: true })` with `.dissolve = 0.55` works |\n89\t| `applySky` every frame, `Mirror`, `RenderView` | No errors, no GPU errors |\n90\t| Music at 10% | Tracks summing to about 1.0 volume: −34 dBFS RMS at 10%, peak 0.91 at 100% (no clipping). The engine recipes' 0.12 + 0.05 volumes would be far too quiet at 10%. |\n91\t| AI Hub harness, shared world, own card, on the engine | All scenarios pass: 2 players; 4 with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; `scenario=host-leave`; `scenario=server` |\n92\t| Serverless WebRTC between two pages on `file://` | Works with `iceServers: []`, even across browser contexts; invite code about 600 characters after deflate + base64. BroadcastChannel works only within one browser context. |\n93\t| Touch profiles (Chromium, Pixel 7, iPad Pro 11) | `touch: false` gives no controls; `settings: { button: 'top-right' }` shows ⚙ and a tap opens the panel |\n94\t| Portrait with the default aspect | Art width collapses to 132 px. Use `aspect: 'fixed'`. |\n95\t| `python3 -m http.server 0` through a pipe | Prints nothing until flushed, so a script waiting for the port hangs. Use `python3 -u`. |\n96\t\n97\t### Layout and packaging\n98\t\n99\t```\n100\tkit/                the engine kit as downloaded, unmodified (check.mjs, docs, packs/)\n101\tvendor/session.js   lib/hub/session.js, unmodified\n102\tsrc/pixel-engine.js byte-for-byte copy of kit/pixel-engine.js\n103\tsrc/engine.js       export * from './pixel-engine.js';   every module imports from here\n104\tsrc/main.js         page entry\n105\ttools/build.mjs     writes the single HTML your TASK.md names\n106\t```\n107\t\n108\t`tools/build.mjs` runs `esbuild src/main.js --bundle --format=esm --target=esnext --external:./pixel-engine.js`, escapes `</script` in the result, and writes:\n109\t\n110\t```html\n111\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n112\t<script>/* vendor/session.js, unmodified: the first script, right after meta charset */</script>\n113\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n114\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\"> <!-- and the other hub tags, section 19 -->\n115\t<title>…</title>\n116\t<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#1a1c2c}#app{position:fixed;inset:0}</style>\n117\t<script>window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__={'packs/kenney-furniture.json':'data:application/json;base64,…','packs/kenney-nature.json':'…'};</script>\n118\t<script id=\"pixel-engine-src\" type=\"text/plain\">/* kit/pixel-engine.js, byte for byte (it contains no </script) */</script>\n119\t<script>(function(){var u=URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([document.getElementById('pixel-engine-src').textContent],{type:'text/javascript'}));var m=document.createElement('script');m.type='importmap';m.textContent=JSON.stringify({imports:{'./pixel-engine.js':u}});document.head.appendChild(m);})();</script>\n120\t</head><body><div id=\"app\"></div><script type=\"module\">/* the esbuild bundle */</script></body></html>\n121\t```\n122\t\n123\tThis keeps the engine exactly as it ships, as TASK.md and the guide require: a test asserts its text appears unchanged in the page. Everything is inline, with no network at runtime. With both packs the page is about 8 MB before your game's code; trimming packs to the models you name is optional. In Node, and if you serve `src/` during development, `./pixel-engine.js` resolves natively.\n124\t\n125\t### Engine start\n126\t\n127\t```js\n128\tconst touchOnly = navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0 && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches;\n129\tEngine.start(game, withUrlOptions({\n130\t  container: document.getElementById('app'),\n131\t  aspect: 'fixed',            // always 480×270: every screen is laid out once\n132\t  quality: 'high',            // 16 pooled lights (session rounds: 'medium')\n133\t  camera: { preset: 'iso', yaw: 45, pitch: 35, zoom: 1.6, minZoom: 1.6, maxZoom: 1.6 },\n134\t  settings: { button: touchOnly ? 'top-right' : false, key: 'your-game-key' },\n135\t  touch: false, touchBar: false, background: PALETTE.ink,\n136\t}));\n137\t```\n138\t\n139\tZoom 1.6 gives 32 art pixels a metre, about 15 m across and 15 m of ground depth. To turn or zoom, tween with `engine.setCamera({ preset:'iso', yaw, pitch:35, zoom:z, minZoom:z, maxZoom:z }, { syncUrl:false })`. Keep movement camera-relative with `ctx.camera.groundBasis()`, so all four turns play the same. Catch a failed start and show a plain full-page message naming the browsers that have WebGPU; in a session call `AIHub.fail(message)` instead.\n140\t\n141\t### Rules this game must follow\n142\t\n143\t- **One `Game` object, one scene, no `loadGame`.** The town, City and interiors are `Group`s at far-apart offsets (town at origin, City at x = 500, interiors from x = 1000), shown one at a time. Doors become `await engine.screen.cover('iris', { center })`, move, `reveal`. Transitions are instant, neighbours keep living while you're indoors, and one recording covers the whole game (a level unload would split replays).\n144\t- **Pause rule.** Never set `engine.paused` or `timeScale = 0` for menus or dialogue. Keep an own `world.frozen` flag that skips simulation; `update` keeps running, so UI animates and water keeps moving.\n145\t- **Movement is our own tile collision**, not Rapier: circle against tile grid plus building rectangles, height from a pure `groundHeight(x, z)`. It's deterministic, fast headless, and trivial to run for four players on a Hub host. Rapier sits idle except for any toy you add.\n146\t- **Gameplay in `fixedUpdate`, presentation in `update`, randomness from `ctx.random` forks, page data through `ctx.input.send`** (guide rule 4): save loads, real-clock readings, typed characters.\n147\t- **Guard `document`, `localStorage`, `drawSheet`** so the game imports in Node. `src/core/storage.js` falls back to memory.\n148\t\n149\t### Input\n150\t\n151\t```js\n152\tinput.gamepadButtons = { 0:'PadCross',1:'PadCircle',2:'PadSquare',3:'PadTriangle',4:'PadL1',5:'PadR1',6:'PadL2',7:'PadR2',8:'PadCreate',9:'PadOptions',10:'PadL3',11:'PadR3',12:'PadUp',13:'PadDown',14:'PadLeft',15:'PadRight',16:'PadPS',17:'PadTouch' };\n153\t```\n154\t\n155\tThe default map presses platformer keys (0 is Space), so replace it. `input.moveAxis()` merges left stick and WASD/arrows. Right stick arrives as `input.mouseDelta`, readable only inside `update` (it resets at frame end). Add `Tab` and any function keys to `input.preventKeys`. For typed text, add your own `keydown` listener and `input.send({ t: 'key', key: e.key })`.\n156\t\n157\t**Last device:** pad when any `Pad*` code is held or pressed or a stick is tilted; keyboard/mouse when another key is pressed or `input.pointer` moved. **Any input** (attract, idle) is the same test.\n158\t\n159\t### Text and UI\n160\t\n161\tWrite your own bitmap font as data, drawn glyph by glyph with `hud.sprite(x, y, rows, { '#': color }, { shadow: false })`: 5×7 caps, lowercase with 2-pixel descenders, proportional, 1 px tracking, 12 px line height. `ink` on a `white` panel with `sand` trim is verified legible at 1080p (cap height 28 screen pixels: the smallest text allowed anywhere).\n162\t\n163\t### Models and rigs\n164\t\n165\t```js\n166\tconst RIG = { joints:['Pelvis','Torso','Head','ArmR','ArmL','LegR','LegL'], root:'Pelvis',\n167\t  mirror:{ArmR:'ArmL',ArmL:'ArmR',LegR:'LegL',LegL:'LegR'}, soles:[], trace:[], limits:{}, fps:30 };\n168\t// in model(): nest { group:[...], at:[x,y,z], pivot:true, name:'Head' } for each joint, Pelvis outermost\n169\tconst obj = buildModel(def, { catalog }).object;\n170\tconst clips = compileClips(CLIP_DEFS, RIG, obj);\n171\tconst mixer = new THREE.AnimationMixer(obj);        // mixer.update(dt) in update()\n172\tmixer.clipAction(clips.find(c => c.name === 'Walk')).play();\n173\t```\n174\t\n175\tEvery critter has all seven joints, even if a snail's legs are empty groups, so every clip validates against every rig. Add extras (`Neck`, `Tail`) by turning them in code after `mixer.update`. Faces are small parts you scale or hide from code (blink, smile, talk). Hair and hats attach to the `Head` group.\n176\t\n177\t**Colours.** `model()` slots take only the 16 palette names. To use an off-palette accent, swap after the build: traverse, and where `mesh.material.color` matches a slot colour, assign `toonMaterial(0xf5a6c0)` [verified]. Allow at most four off-palette colours in the whole game (blossom pink is one). The same swap puts a pattern texture on a shirt: `toonMaterial(PALETTE.white, { map })`.\n178\t\n179\t**Library notes.**\n180\t- `kenney-furniture` is near real size (bed 1.14×0.75×2.25 m, sofa 1.96×0.92×0.82 m); its `plant-small*` are tiny, so scale them. Slots: `wood metal metalLight metalDark carpet carpetDarker lamp plant`. Its defaults are nearly all orange and sand, so **recolour every set through `palette`**.\n181\t- `kenney-nature` has `dirt grass stone` slots. Its orange trunks look wrong: remap them, or write your own trees (recommended, for seasons and fruit).\n182\t\n183\t### Surfaces\n184\t\n185\tThe ground is most of the screen and decides whether characters read. **The library's `meadow`, `flagstones` and `snow-drifts` are too noisy at this zoom** [verified by render]. Write quiet ones: a flat base plus sparse specks.\n186\t\n187\t```js\n188\tconst full = c => ({ path: 'M0 0H32V32H0Z', fill: c });   // without a base layer the gaps render BLACK\n189\texport const lawn = textureDef({ id:'lawn', about:'Quiet lawn', tags:['surface'], size:[32,32], tile:true,\n190\t  palette:{ a:'green', b:'lime' }, layers:[ full('a'), { pattern:'scatter', colors:['b'], count:9, size:1, seed:4 } ] });\n191\tTEXTURE_LIBRARY.add(lawn);\n192\t```\n193\t\n194\tRendered and approved: summer `green`+`lime`; spring `lime`+`green`+`white`; winter `white`+`mist`. **Autumn grass and paths must differ:** sand grass needs an orange path; winter paths are `mist`.\n195\t\n196\t### Occluders\n197\t\n198\tEvery frame, test the camera-to-player line against the bounding boxes of trees and buildings. Swap hits to shared dissolve materials (a small cache per colour, `.dissolve = 0.55`), and swap back with a little hysteresis. Don't clone per object.\n199\t\n200\t---\n201\t\n202\t## 3. Time, calendar, weather\n203\t\n204\t**Two clocks,** chosen on the first run and changeable in Settings:\n205\t\n206\t- **Village Time (default).** 1 game minute = 0.8 real seconds, so a full day takes about 19 minutes. The clock stops during dialogue, menus and shop screens. Day One runs at half speed until the errands are done.\n207\t- **Real Clock.** The device's time and date, like the source. Season from month (Mar–May spring, Jun–Aug summer, Sep–Nov autumn, Dec–Feb winter); Sunday is Market Day, Saturday is Show Day, other days are Errand Days; the contest falls on the second Wednesday of the season's middle month, the festival on its last Saturday. Story beats follow days played, not dates. Readings arrive through `input.send`.\n208\t\n209\t**Calendar.** Four seasons of four days each, sixteen days a year: **Market Day, Errand Day, Show Day, Festival Day**. New day at 05:00. The HUD reads \"Spring · Market Day · 9:42 AM\". After Day 16 the year rolls over with the same calendar.\n210\t\n211\t**Bed** saves and offers: *Sleep till morning · Nap till noon · Nap till dusk · Hibernate until the next festival* (with a scolding from Mrs. Gander). Hibernate lets anyone reach every season without dev mode.\n212\t\n213\t**Season change** happens overnight behind a full-screen almanac page turning, with a title card and a sting. Reskin ground, canopies and props under cover (filter changes rebuild the pipeline once, so hide that too).\n214\t\n215\t| Season | Ground | Trees | Air |\n216\t|---|---|---|---|\n217\t| Spring | lime, white specks | blossom pink, then lime | petals, showers, rainbow after rain |\n218\t| Summer | green | deep green | fireflies, cicadas, thunder evenings (`screen.flash` + rumble) |\n219\t| Autumn | sand, orange specks | orange, red, sand | blowing leaves, mushrooms, ground fog (`groundFog`) |\n220\t| Winter | white, snow footprints (`Decals`) | bare or snow-capped | `Precipitation('snow')`, frosted windows, snowballs |\n221\t\n222\t**Weather** is seeded per day with an hourly start and stop: spring sun 60 / rain 30 / fog 10; summer sun 70 / rain 20 / storm 10; autumn sun 50 / wind 30 / rain 20; winter snow 60 / clear 40. Rain waters flowers, wakes snails, fills puddles, brings out umbrellas, and switches the music variant.\n223\t\n224\t**Day and night.** `applySky(engine, skyAt(hour))` each game minute. `skyAt(h).night` drives windows (glow parts on at 0.35), lamp lights (`ctx.lights`), fireflies, stars.\n225\t\n226\t**Absence** (both clocks, using a real timestamp kept in the save). After a real day or more away: +3 weeds per day (cap 40), 1–3 **dust bunnies** per day in your home (tiny, cowardly, net or stomp them, cap 8), mail piling up, and a first-talk line from each neighbour (\"Oh. You're alive. Dorrit owes me a pie.\"). Flowers wilt but don't die. Nothing is lost.\n227\t\n228\t---\n229\t\n230\t## 4. The village\n231\t\n232\tAn 80×64-tile map (1 tile = 1 m), five acres by four, each 16×16, authored as rows of characters in `src/data/townMap.js`. Tests flood-fill it: every door, stop and activity must be reachable. Fronts and doors face south or east, so the default camera sees them. North is cliff and waterfall; south is the sea. The map is original: don't reproduce any source town.\n233\t\n234\t| | Col 1 | Col 2 | Col 3 | Col 4 | Col 5 |\n235\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n236\t| **A** (upper terrace, +2 m) | Pine wood | **Museum** | Waterfall and pool | Pear orchard, ramp | **Lookout** bench (stargazing) |\n237\t| **B** | **Bus stop**, road out | Dorrit, Tavi, Nib; ramp up | River, north bridge | Bartleby, Ottoline; ramp up | Mossy wood, Rocco |\n238\t| **C** | **Town Hall & Post** | **Plaza**: Old Pear tree, Old Bell, notice board, benches | River bend, south bridge, pond | **Mrs. Gander's store**, **Mothwick's** | **Your plot**, Ozzie, Yolanda |\n239\t| **D** (beach) | Tide pools | Dock | River mouth | Lighthouse | Palm grove, driftwood |\n240\t\n241\t- **Hide the map's edges on all four camera turns:** a deep forest ring outside the walkable area on three sides, sea to the south.\n242\t- **Terrain:** 16×16 chunks of merged meshes, one per surface, rebuilt when a tile changes. Cliff faces are textured boxes. One big `WaterSurface` under terrain with holes where water tiles are (`foamAt` high, so foam stays rare).\n243\t- **Worn paths:** tiles walked often turn to path and heal slowly. The village shapes itself to you.\n244\t- **Wind:** `swayObject` on canopies and grasses with one shared `WindUniforms`.\n245\t- **Starting population:** about 55 trees, 6 rocks, flowers around homes, driftwood, shells, clothesline, chimneys that smoke.\n246\t\n247\t---\n248\t\n249\t## 5. You: avatar, movement, tools\n250\t\n251\t**Avatar.** A small round person of no particular species, and a running joke (\"What *are* you? No, don't tell me.\"). About 1.5 m to the crown, head about 55% of height: the proportions verified as readable at zoom 1.6. Eyes (6), hair (8) in colours (8), outfit, hat. Set by the bus conversation (section 13), then changed at the salon and mirror.\n252\t\n253\t**Movement.** Tiptoe 1.2 m/s (light tilt or Ctrl), walk 2.6, jog 4.2 (full tilt or plain WASD), run 6.0 (hold Circle/Shift). Running wears paths, spooks bugs and tramples flowers. Quick turns, squash on stopping, dust at speed. Pressing the tool button with empty hands gets a once-per-session gag: *\"You can't jump. You're in escrow.\"*\n254\t\n255\t**Targeting.** The target is the interactable within 1.2 m in a 70° cone, else the tile under `position + facing × 0.9`. A bouncing pixel chevron marks it; a chunky highlight marks the tile; a context prompt shows in the corner. With a mouse, hover drives all of this instead.\n256\t\n257\t**Tools** (equipped one at a time; L1/R1, wheel or 1–6):\n258\t\n259\t| Tool | Use |\n260\t|---|---|\n261\t| Net | Press to ready (holds pose, slows you), release to sweep: 1.8 m reach, 90° cone |\n262\t| Rod | Cast at water; press again to reel (section 6) |\n263\t| Shovel | Dig, refill, bury, hit rocks |\n264\t| Axe | Three hits fell a tree; shovel removes the stump |\n265\t| Watering can | Waters the target tile and its neighbours |\n266\t| Slingshot | Shoots balloons; pad auto-aims the nearest ahead, mouse aims |\n267\t\n268\t**Bare-hand verbs:** talk, pick up, shake a tree, pull a weed, pick a flower, knock, sit, read, push a snowball. **Pockets:** 15 slots.\n269\t\n270\t**Reveal.** A first catch or find plays the hold-up pose, a jingle and its line (\"You caught a pike! Someone should tell it about the other end of the hook.\"). Repeats are quick.\n271\t\n272\t---\n273\t\n274\t## 6. Things to do\n275\t\n276\t**Fishing.** Shadows (S, M, L, XL) drift in water tiles (3 per water acre). Cast lands a bobber; a nearby shadow turns and comes. **1–3 false nibbles** (small dip, plip, faint rumble), then the **bite**: hard plunge, splash, \"!\", strong rumble. Reel window 0.6 s; miss and it flees. No other penalty.\n277\t\n278\t**Bugs.** About 1.5 per acre, by season, hour, weather and spot. Spook radius: run 3 m, jog 2, walk 1.2, tiptoe 0.5. Some sit on trunks, flowers, lamps or rocks. Hitting a rock can release a pill bug.\n279\t\n280\t| Fish (floor 14 → target 18) | Beans | Where | When | Line |\n281\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n282\t| Minnow | 60 | river | not winter | \"That's… that's all of it.\" |\n283\t| Perch | 150 | river | all, day | \"It was just sitting there. On purpose.\" |\n284\t| Crawfish | 200 | pond | spring, summer | \"It has opinions about your thumbs.\" |\n285\t| Carp | 300 | pond | all | \"Carp diem! …Sorry. It made me say that.\" |\n286\t| Trout | 400 | river | spring, autumn | \"It isn't pouting. That's just its face.\" |\n287\t| Catfish | 800 | pond | summer, autumn, night | \"It claims to be a different catfish online.\" |\n288\t| Pike | 900 | river | autumn, winter | \"Someone should tell it about the other end of the hook.\" |\n289\t| Eel | 1,200 | river | summer, autumn, dusk on | \"Slippery. Also, it knows what you did.\" |\n290\t| Salmon | 1,500 | river mouth | autumn | \"It came all this way and now look.\" |\n291\t| Golden Carp | 5,000 | pond | spring, dawn, rare | \"It looks expensive and it knows.\" |\n292\t| Sturgeon | 6,000 | river mouth | winter, rare | \"Older than the Mayor. Slightly faster.\" |\n293\t| Sardine | 100 | sea | all | \"Comes with its own sense of confinement.\" |\n294\t| Mackerel | 250 | sea | summer, autumn | \"Holy it.\" |\n295\t| Sea Bream | 500 | sea | spring, summer | \"Very calm. Suspiciously calm.\" |\n296\t| Flounder | 700 | sea | all | \"Both eyes on you. Literally.\" |\n297\t| Pufferfish | 1,800 | sea | summer, dusk on | \"It's taking this very personally.\" |\n298\t| Moonfish | 4,000 | sea | summer, autumn, late, rare | \"It's bigger than your whole debt. Almost.\" |\n299\t| Old Boot | 10 | anywhere | any (not for the museum) | \"Somewhere, a fish is walking funny.\" |\n300\t\n301\t| Bug (floor 14 → target 18) | Beans | Where | When | Line |\n302\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n303\t| Cabbage Butterfly | 90 | flowers | spring, summer, day | \"It's very sorry about the cabbage.\" |\n304\t| Pill Bug | 100 | under rocks | all | \"It has rolled up and refused to comment.\" |\n305\t| Ladybird | 120 | flowers | spring | \"It came for the aphids and stayed for the drama.\" |\n306\t| Pond Skater | 130 | water | summer | \"Technically walking on water. Not technically impressed.\" |\n307\t| Cricket | 130 | grass | autumn, night | \"It was just getting to the good part.\" |\n308\t| Moth | 150 | lamps | all, night | \"It has found the lamp. It has found its purpose.\" |\n309\t| Grasshopper | 160 | grass | summer, autumn | \"It didn't save anything for winter either.\" |\n310\t| Dragonfly | 180 | near water | summer, autumn | \"Four wings and still somehow caught.\" |\n311\t| Bumblebee | 200 | flowers | spring, summer | \"It's too busy for this, and it says so.\" |\n312\t| Snail | 200 | rocks, rain only | spring, summer | \"Not the Mayor. Probably.\" |\n313\t| Cicada | 250 | trunks | summer, day | \"Seventeen years underground for this.\" |\n314\t| Swallowtail | 300 | flowers | summer, day | \"Fancy. It wants you to know that.\" |\n315\t| Firefly | 300 | by water | summer, night | \"Dimmer up close. Aren't we all.\" |\n316\t| Woolly Bear | 350 | trunks | autumn | \"Yolanda wants to know where it got that sweater.\" |\n317\t| Praying Mantis | 400 | flowers | summer, autumn | \"It isn't praying. It's negotiating.\" |\n318\t| Snow Flea | 600 | snow | winter, midday | \"Technically a bug. Emotionally, a snowflake.\" |\n319\t| Stag Beetle | 2,000 | trunks | summer, night, rare | \"It has been waiting for someone to try that.\" |\n320\t| Rhino Beetle | 2,500 | trunks | summer, late, rare | \"You're not sure who caught whom.\" |\n321\t\n322\t**Digging.** Three star-shaped cracks a day (`Decals` `crack`) hide fossils; a sparkle spot hides a bag of beans. Bags can be **buried and grown into a beanstalk** in 2 days, tripling them (\"Beans are currency, dear. You can't plant them.\" \"You can absolutely plant them. You didn't hear it from me.\"). One rock a day pays out when struck.\n323\t\n324\t**Trees.** Shake to drop what's in the canopy: pears (100 beans, regrow in 2 days), one tree a day has a bag or furniture, one has a wasp nest. Wasps chase you; a sting means a puffy-eye face until medicine or morning, and neighbours comment. Foreign fruit (500) comes from Gran's letters and can be planted. Saplings take 3 days, with a visual stage each day.\n325\t\n326\t**Flowers.** Four species, 3–5 colours. Plant, water, and hybrids appear beside touching same-species pairs that were watered (15% a night). Unwatered flowers wilt in 2 days. **Weeds:** 3 a day; pull by hand; neighbours notice.\n327\t\n328\t**Balloons.** Three a day drift over with a present on a string. Slingshot it; it falls somewhere awkward.\n329\t\n330\t**Beachcombing.** Six new shells a day, 15–300 beans, plus driftwood oddities.\n331\t\n332\t**Stargazing.** Sit on the Lookout bench at night: the camera glides up (`fixed` preset, `Timeline`) to a star field with six original constellations you can name in the Almanac (\"The Overdue Library Book\", \"The Big Spoon\"). Press on a **shooting star** to wish; a present arrives in the morning.\n333\t\n334\t**Snow.** In winter, push a snowball and it grows. Stack two (body, head) near each other: a snowman appears, comments on the proportions, and mails a gift if they were good.\n335\t\n336\t---\n337\t\n338\t## 7. Money\n339\t\n340\t**Beans** are the currency. Plenty of jokes: a hill of beans, spilling them, the bean counter.\n341\t\n342\t**Debt.** Mrs. Gander's terms, payable at the store, never enforced, never forgotten.\n343\t\n344\t| Home tier | Added debt | Result |\n345\t|---|---|---|\n346\t| 1 Starter shack | 8,000 | One 6×6 room |\n347\t| 2 Proper cottage | 30,000 | One 8×8 room |\n348\t| 3 Cottage with annex | 80,000 | Plus a 6×6 side room |\n349\t| 4 Two storeys | 150,000 | Plus an 8×8 upstairs, and a plaque |\n350\t\n351\tFinishing tier 4 earns a gold-edged deed and Mrs. Gander's first compliment, which she immediately regrets. A diligent player clears tier 4 around Day 16 (expected income: 1–3k on Day 1, about 15k a day by midgame). Paint jobs (roof and wall colours) cost extra.\n352\t\n353\t**Store.** Mrs. Gander's, three tiers by lifetime spend (25k, 120k), with a new exterior each. Daily rotating stock is shown **as real objects on real shelves**: walk up, press to get price and yes/no. To sell, talk to her: your Pockets open and she quotes as you browse, no table. Stock: 2 furniture, 2 clothes, 1 wallpaper, 1 floor, tools, seeds, stationery, \"today's oddity\". Hours 8:00–22:00.\n354\t\n355\t**Cheese market** (our own speculative game). **Old Rind**, a whispering stoat, sells cheese wheels at the plaza on Market Day mornings (06:00–12:00) for 90–110 beans. Prices change at 08:00 and 14:00 on the next three days; pattern per season: Rising, Falling, Wobble or Spike (peak 3–6×), shown on the notice board. Wheels go bad at the next Market Day (then worth 1 bean, with a visible smell). Sell at the store.\n356\t\n357\t**Bean Jar** (Town Hall). Deposit and withdraw; 0.5% interest per season (\"We are very interested.\"). **Lost & Found** bin: leave things, find things. **Catalogue:** everything you've owned can be reordered at Post on big one-item cards; it arrives by mail next day. **Selling a gift a neighbour gave you** works, and they will hear about it.\n358\t\n359\t---\n360\t\n361\t## 8. Home\n362\t\n363\t- **Interior:** a dollhouse cutaway with two back walls, one iso camera, no turns [look verified]. Grid 1 m; items 1×1, 2×1, 2×2.\n364\t- **Placement mode:** ✕ lifts or drops, L1/R1 rotates, mouse drags. Wallpaper and floor apply from Pockets. Items can't block the door.\n365\t- **Interactive furniture (must work):** bed; chairs and sofas you sit on; lamps on/off (real pooled lights); **stereo** plays your collected tunes; **TV** shows a live feed of the plaza (`RenderView`, attached only while you're home); **mirror** really reflects (`Mirror`) and opens the wardrobe; fish tank and bug cage show what you put in; clock; toilet flushes pointlessly.\n366\t- **Furniture:** floor 40 → target 60, in sets: Cottage, Mossy, Seaside, City Loft, Bureau, and the absurd Cheese set, plus singles. Most are `kenney-furniture` recoloured through `palette`; the signature pieces are your own models.\n367\t- **Exterior:** one model per tier, mailbox with a flag up when there's mail.\n368\t\n369\t**Better Burrows Bureau rating** (section 11). Scored on Show Day nights, result by mail: points for item value, set bonuses (4+ matching), colour harmony, \"cozy rules\" (lamp by a seat, rug under a table, something growing), music playing, minus clutter. Grades C, B, A, S with a sentence of critique: *\"Too many chairs. Who are you expecting?\"* Reward furniture at each new grade.\n370\t\n371\t---\n372\t\n373\t## 9. Neighbours\n374\t\n375\t| Name | Species | Type | Tic | Home | Loves / hates |\n376\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n377\t| **Dorrit** | wombat | Hearth | \"…mmhm\" | Kitchen, too many pots | soup, rain / loud hats |\n378\t| **Tavi** | quokka | Sunshine | \"isn't it?!\" | Everything yellow | everyone / nothing (it's worrying) |\n379\t| **Bartleby** | moose | Grouch | \"I'd prefer not.\" | Bare room, one good chair; laundry stuck on his antlers | quiet, pine / being asked |\n380\t| **Lady Ottoline** | platypus | Diva | \"darling dear\" | Faded grandeur, moth-eaten rug | pearls, flattery / mud (she lives in mud) |\n381\t| **Rocco** | armadillo | Go-Getter | \"let's roll!\" | Gym, trophies he awarded himself | protein, fish / stairs |\n382\t| **Nib** | lemur | Dreamer | \"…or so the moon says\" | Books, telescope, loose paper | stars, bugs / alarms |\n383\t| **Ozzie** | possum | Schemer | \"You didn't hear it from me.\" | Corkboard and red string; plays dead when surprised | cheese, boxes / Mrs. Gander |\n384\t| **Yolanda** | yak | Auntie | \"hon\" | Everything knitted, including the TV | gossip, wool / secrets she wasn't told |\n385\t| **Fitch** (moves in, Day 9) | ferret | Hustler | \"no refunds\" | Half-unpacked boxes | deals / questions |\n386\t\n387\t**Relationships.** Tavi keeps waving at Bartleby. Ozzie suspects Mrs. Gander of everything. Ottoline and Yolanda are locked in a feud about a scarf. Dorrit feeds everyone. Rocco is training Nib against Nib's will. Lines reference these.\n388\t\n389\t**Routines.** Wake and sleep by type. Daily points of interest: plaza benches, riverbank (they fish, badly), store, museum, each other's doors. Rain: umbrellas or indoors. Night: home, window lit; knock after bedtime and get a sleepy complaint. Paths come from `NavGrid`. Two neighbours chat with bubbles; walk within 2 m to read a piece of it. They hum, sit, stare at bugs, carry things.\n390\t\n391\t**Friendship** 0–100 (stranger, acquaintance, friend, best friend). First talk of the day +3, favour +5–10, gift +2–10 by taste, good letter +3. Hitting someone with the net −2 (it does happen, they do comment).\n392\t\n393\t**Memory** is what sells it. Keep `memory` events: gifts, catches, sales, outfits, net bonks, absences. Lines pull from them: *\"Heard you sold the scarf Yolanda knitted you. To the goose. She's not mad. She's knitting about it.\"*\n394\t\n395\t**Favours.** Two a day, marked with a \"!\" over their head: fetch a named item, deliver a parcel, catch something within an hour, return a lost thing, trade. Floor 12 → target 20 templates, flavoured per type.\n396\t\n397\t**Letters.** Mail arrives at 09:00: neighbour letters (with presents, per friendship), Gran's passive-aggressive note each season with a foreign fruit or something inexplicable, museum thanks, Bureau results, catalogue orders, the occasional anonymous tip from Ozzie. You write letters at Post, on paper, with an attached gift, using the on-screen keyboard or typing. Replies next day react to what you wrote: their name present, length, gift. *\"You wrote 'asdfgh'. I framed it.\"*\n398\t\n399\t**Move-out and move-in.** A complete bittersweet beat: Rocco talks about leaving on Day 7 (you may argue, and it changes his goodbye), boxes appear, he's gone on Day 9 with a goodbye letter and, if a best friend, his favourite trophy. Fitch moves into his house that same morning. Rocco writes later and rolls back to visit on the last night. Dev mode can trigger any move.\n400\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7fc0c99-6050-4a36-bbad-13de512b63e9","uuid":"5199c2b0-3cc6-4be6-83b7-da2f440aa2fa","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:18:28.272Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Game Plan: a village life sim, one bus ride from the Big City\n\nSource: *Animal Crossing: City Folk*. You build an **original** game inspired by it, from this plan and your TASK.md, without its author. Read TASK.md first, then this whole file once.\n\n**Contents:** 0 Using this plan · 1 The game · 2 Engine and packaging (verified recipes) · 3 Time, calendar, weather · 4 The village · 5 You: avatar, movement, tools · 6 Things to do · 7 Money · 8 Home · 9 Neighbours · 10 Town services · 11 The City · 12 The year · 13 Day One · 14 Writing bible · 15 UI and UX · 16 Art direction · 17 Animation · 18 Audio · 19 Multiplayer · 20 Title, attract, pause, settings, dev mode, About, saves · 21 Code architecture · 22 Testing · 23 Build order and cut rules · 24 Finish checklist and TASK.md coverage\n\n---\n\n## 0. Using this plan\n\n- **Title.** It is yours to choose, the way TASK.md says: brainstorm silently, pick with a real random number generator, do it first. This plan says \"the game\" throughout. The riff must stand on its own joke (see *Originality* in section 1).\n- **Names and paths.** TASK.md names your deliverable HTML, your dev folder and your exit interview file. This plan never does. Every path below is relative to your dev folder.\n- **[verified]** means the author ran it on engine kit 0.46.0, on this machine, before writing this. Treat it as known good. If your kit is newer and something differs, read the kit's `CHANGELOG.md`.\n- **Numbers are defaults.** They live in one tuning table (section 20). Change them when play shows they're wrong.\n- **Content counts are written floor → target.** Every system ships. Under pressure, content may drop to its floor; a system never ships half-built (section 23).\n- **Changing a decision:** you may, if it fails in practice. Keep the pillar it served.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** You step off the last bus at the end of the line and, within ninety seconds, owe a goose 8,000 beans. The village is tiny, its neighbours are odd and opinionated, and every day has something to catch, dig, grow, sell, overhear or regret. One bus ride away, the Big City glows: salons, auctions, couture, a theatre, and a magpie selling art that is \"only forty percent stolen\". The village keeps time whether you're there or not, and it will mention it.\n\n| Field | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| **Genre** | Life sim / social sim (AI Hub genre: `Life Sim`) |\n| **Perspective** | Isometric 3/4 orthographic diorama: engine `iso` preset, yaw 45°, pitch 35°, zoom 1.6, with 90° camera turns. Same camera indoors, as a dollhouse cutaway. Chosen from side-by-side renders: straight-on (yaw 0) reads flat and roofs turn into colour blocks; 45° reads like a tabletop model and suits the engine's pixel pipeline. |\n| **Length** | A 16-day calendar year, about 2.5 to 5 hours to live fully; most features are reachable in the first hour; a player can sleep ahead to any season without dev mode. Play continues after the ending. |\n| **Players** | 1 solo; 1–4 in AI Hub sessions; 1–4 visiting on its own (section 19) |\n\n**What makes the source distinctive, and our version of it:**\n\n| The source's essence | Our version |\n|---|---|\n| The world runs on a clock and calendar, with or without you | Village Time or Real Clock, a different tune every hour, weeds and letters that pile up, neighbours who noticed you left |\n| A small town of animal neighbours with personality and gossip | Eight odd misfits with tics, routines, feuds and a memory of what you did |\n| Cheerful debt to a shopkeeper who owns everything | Mrs. Gander, a terrifyingly courteous goose and the village's bean counter |\n| Collect everything, donate to a museum | Fish, bugs, fossils and art, one pun at a time, for a near-sighted curator |\n| City Folk's signature: a bus to a city of shops | The Big City: bus ballads, neon nights, salon, couture fashion check, auction, theatre, shady art dealer, home-rating bureau, fortunes, café, crowds |\n| Visiting friends' towns | AI Hub round, and Visit mode on its own |\n| Warm, deadpan writing and babbling voices | Writing bible (section 14), code-made gibberish voices |\n| Seasonal holidays | Four festivals, three contests, concerts, snow |\n| Self-expression | Pixel patterns, a village chime you compose, furniture sets, outfits, hair |\n\n**Pillars**\n\n1. **The village has a pulse.** It keeps time, remembers and carries on.\n2. **Neighbours are the game.** They should be the thing a player tells a friend about.\n3. **Small verbs, big satisfaction.** Shake, swing, cast, dig. Each has anticipation, impact, a sound, a sparkle and usually a joke.\n4. **Two worlds, one bus.** Home is slow and soft. The City is loud and lit. The ride between them is a threshold you feel.\n5. **Nothing is urgent.** No failure, no combat, no game over, no stamina. The debt is a running joke. In Village Time the clock stops while you talk or browse.\n6. **Ten feet away.** Big type, few choices on screen, everything within two presses.\n\n**Not in the game:** combat, stamina, terraforming, touch controls, voice or text chat, couch split-screen.\n\n**Originality (hard rules).** TASK.md asks for an original game inspired by the source. Genre and mechanics are fair game. Its names, characters, art, music, maps and look are not.\n\n- No character, name or term from the series: no Nook, Bells, Gyroids, K.K., Resetti, Happy Home Academy, Able Sisters, Stalk Market or turnips.\n- No species-for-role copies: no raccoon or tanuki shopkeeper, owl curator, mole scold, dog musician, hedgehog tailors, kappa driver, shih tzu clerk, fox art dealer, poodle stylist, giraffe couturier.\n- Neighbours are species the series' villager roster doesn't use (wombat, quokka, moose, platypus…), which also gives the game its own silhouette.\n- No melody from the series, no imitation of a recognisable tune. All music is newly composed.\n- No copying of its character designs or UI layouts. No rolling-log curved horizon.\n- The title is a pun that works by itself, not the source's name with a letter changed; its logo never carries the source's name.\n- The source is named only where TASK.md requires it: About/Credits and the catalogue fields.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Engine and packaging: verified recipes\n\nThe kit is a WebGPU engine that renders chunky low-poly 3D as pixel art at 480×270. Read the kit's `GUIDE.md` whole and `ASSETS.md` whole; search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`. What follows is what those docs don't tell you for *this* game.\n\n### What was tested\n\n| Question | Result [verified] |\n|---|---|\n| **Browser flags for WebGPU here** | **The engine needs `--use-angle=swiftshader`.** With only `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`, as TASK.md lists them, it never renders: the device is lost (`Instance dropped in popErrorScope`), `gpuRecoveries` climbs, the frame count sticks, the picture stays blank. Use the kit's own `check.mjs` set: `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`. |\n| One HTML on `file://`, WebGPU on | Works. Boots in 1–2 s on the software GPU. Inline modules avoid the `file://` module ban the guide warns about. |\n| Engine byte-identical inside the page | Works, including inside Hub harness iframes: recipe below |\n| Packs in one file | `loadPack('packs/<id>.json')` works from `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__` data URLs |\n| Custom character rig | `buildModel` pivots + `compileClips` + `THREE.AnimationMixer` work. `crossFadeTo` blends. `Animator`, `PoseLayers` and `restPoseOf` are **not** exported by the kit; `validateClip`, `renderSheet`, `gaitClip`, `SpringChain`, `Squash` are. |\n| `Engine.headless` in Node | Runs a scene with water, grass, weather, packs and custom rigs. 3,600 steps took 54 ms on a small scene. `hud` works there with `canvas: null`. |\n| Mixed-case text via `hud.sprite` | Legible at 1080p. 3,000 glyphs a frame changing every frame showed no measurable slowdown. (`hud.text` is uppercase only.) |\n| `engine.paused = true` | **The game's `update` stops, so a menu drawn there dies.** `timeScale = 0` keeps `update` running with `dt = 0`. See the pause rule below. |\n| Rotating or zooming the iso camera live | `engine.setCamera({...}, { syncUrl: false })` every frame is cheap (31 calls: 3 ms) |\n| Wheel for tools instead of zoom | `minZoom === maxZoom` locks the engine's wheel and +/− zoom; `input.wheel` still reads in `update` |\n| Mouse | `input.pointer` tracks hover with no button held; `camera.rayAt` + a ground plane gives the tile |\n| Stub DualSense | `input.gamepadButtons` with custom codes works; `isDown('PadCross')` true, `input.snapshot()` shows it |\n| See-through occluders | Swapping a mesh to a shared `toonMaterial(c, { dissolve: true })` with `.dissolve = 0.55` works |\n| `applySky` every frame, `Mirror`, `RenderView` | No errors, no GPU errors |\n| Music at 10% | Tracks summing to about 1.0 volume: −34 dBFS RMS at 10%, peak 0.91 at 100% (no clipping). The engine recipes' 0.12 + 0.05 volumes would be far too quiet at 10%. |\n| AI Hub harness, shared world, own card, on the engine | All scenarios pass: 2 players; 4 with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; `scenario=host-leave`; `scenario=server` |\n| Serverless WebRTC between two pages on `file://` | Works with `iceServers: []`, even across browser contexts; invite code about 600 characters after deflate + base64. BroadcastChannel works only within one browser context. |\n| Touch profiles (Chromium, Pixel 7, iPad Pro 11) | `touch: false` gives no controls; `settings: { button: 'top-right' }` shows ⚙ and a tap opens the panel |\n| Portrait with the default aspect | Art width collapses to 132 px. Use `aspect: 'fixed'`. |\n| `python3 -m http.server 0` through a pipe | Prints nothing until flushed, so a script waiting for the port hangs. Use `python3 -u`. |\n\n### Layout and packaging\n\n```\nkit/                the engine kit as downloaded, unmodified (check.mjs, docs, packs/)\nvendor/session.js   lib/hub/session.js, unmodified\nsrc/pixel-engine.js byte-for-byte copy of kit/pixel-engine.js\nsrc/engine.js       export * from './pixel-engine.js';   every module imports from here\nsrc/main.js         page entry\ntools/build.mjs     writes the single HTML your TASK.md names\n```\n\n`tools/build.mjs` runs `esbuild src/main.js --bundle --format=esm --target=esnext --external:./pixel-engine.js`, escapes `</script` in the result, and writes:\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/* vendor/session.js, unmodified: the first script, right after meta charset */</script>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\"> <!-- and the other hub tags, section 19 -->\n<title>…</title>\n<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#1a1c2c}#app{position:fixed;inset:0}</style>\n<script>window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__={'packs/kenney-furniture.json':'data:application/json;base64,…','packs/kenney-nature.json':'…'};</script>\n<script id=\"pixel-engine-src\" type=\"text/plain\">/* kit/pixel-engine.js, byte for byte (it contains no </script) */</script>\n<script>(function(){var u=URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([document.getElementById('pixel-engine-src').textContent],{type:'text/javascript'}));var m=document.createElement('script');m.type='importmap';m.textContent=JSON.stringify({imports:{'./pixel-engine.js':u}});document.head.appendChild(m);})();</script>\n</head><body><div id=\"app\"></div><script type=\"module\">/* the esbuild bundle */</script></body></html>\n```\n\nThis keeps the engine exactly as it ships, as TASK.md and the guide require: a test asserts its text appears unchanged in the page. Everything is inline, with no network at runtime. With both packs the page is about 8 MB before your game's code; trimming packs to the models you name is optional. In Node, and if you serve `src/` during development, `./pixel-engine.js` resolves natively.\n\n### Engine start\n\n```js\nconst touchOnly = navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0 && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches;\nEngine.start(game, withUrlOptions({\n  container: document.getElementById('app'),\n  aspect: 'fixed',            // always 480×270: every screen is laid out once\n  quality: 'high',            // 16 pooled lights (session rounds: 'medium')\n  camera: { preset: 'iso', yaw: 45, pitch: 35, zoom: 1.6, minZoom: 1.6, maxZoom: 1.6 },\n  settings: { button: touchOnly ? 'top-right' : false, key: 'your-game-key' },\n  touch: false, touchBar: false, background: PALETTE.ink,\n}));\n```\n\nZoom 1.6 gives 32 art pixels a metre, about 15 m across and 15 m of ground depth. To turn or zoom, tween with `engine.setCamera({ preset:'iso', yaw, pitch:35, zoom:z, minZoom:z, maxZoom:z }, { syncUrl:false })`. Keep movement camera-relative with `ctx.camera.groundBasis()`, so all four turns play the same. Catch a failed start and show a plain full-page message naming the browsers that have WebGPU; in a session call `AIHub.fail(message)` instead.\n\n### Rules this game must follow\n\n- **One `Game` object, one scene, no `loadGame`.** The town, City and interiors are `Group`s at far-apart offsets (town at origin, City at x = 500, interiors from x = 1000), shown one at a time. Doors become `await engine.screen.cover('iris', { center })`, move, `reveal`. Transitions are instant, neighbours keep living while you're indoors, and one recording covers the whole game (a level unload would split replays).\n- **Pause rule.** Never set `engine.paused` or `timeScale = 0` for menus or dialogue. Keep an own `world.frozen` flag that skips simulation; `update` keeps running, so UI animates and water keeps moving.\n- **Movement is our own tile collision**, not Rapier: circle against tile grid plus building rectangles, height from a pure `groundHeight(x, z)`. It's deterministic, fast headless, and trivial to run for four players on a Hub host. Rapier sits idle except for any toy you add.\n- **Gameplay in `fixedUpdate`, presentation in `update`, randomness from `ctx.random` forks, page data through `ctx.input.send`** (guide rule 4): save loads, real-clock readings, typed characters.\n- **Guard `document`, `localStorage`, `drawSheet`** so the game imports in Node. `src/core/storage.js` falls back to memory.\n\n### Input\n\n```js\ninput.gamepadButtons = { 0:'PadCross',1:'PadCircle',2:'PadSquare',3:'PadTriangle',4:'PadL1',5:'PadR1',6:'PadL2',7:'PadR2',8:'PadCreate',9:'PadOptions',10:'PadL3',11:'PadR3',12:'PadUp',13:'PadDown',14:'PadLeft',15:'PadRight',16:'PadPS',17:'PadTouch' };\n```\n\nThe default map presses platformer keys (0 is Space), so replace it. `input.moveAxis()` merges left stick and WASD/arrows. Right stick arrives as `input.mouseDelta`, readable only inside `update` (it resets at frame end). Add `Tab` and any function keys to `input.preventKeys`. For typed text, add your own `keydown` listener and `input.send({ t: 'key', key: e.key })`.\n\n**Last device:** pad when any `Pad*` code is held or pressed or a stick is tilted; keyboard/mouse when another key is pressed or `input.pointer` moved. **Any input** (attract, idle) is the same test.\n\n### Text and UI\n\nWrite your own bitmap font as data, drawn glyph by glyph with `hud.sprite(x, y, rows, { '#': color }, { shadow: false })`: 5×7 caps, lowercase with 2-pixel descenders, proportional, 1 px tracking, 12 px line height. `ink` on a `white` panel with `sand` trim is verified legible at 1080p (cap height 28 screen pixels: the smallest text allowed anywhere).\n\n### Models and rigs\n\n```js\nconst RIG = { joints:['Pelvis','Torso','Head','ArmR','ArmL','LegR','LegL'], root:'Pelvis',\n  mirror:{ArmR:'ArmL',ArmL:'ArmR',LegR:'LegL',LegL:'LegR'}, soles:[], trace:[], limits:{}, fps:30 };\n// in model(): nest { group:[...], at:[x,y,z], pivot:true, name:'Head' } for each joint, Pelvis outermost\nconst obj = buildModel(def, { catalog }).object;\nconst clips = compileClips(CLIP_DEFS, RIG, obj);\nconst mixer = new THREE.AnimationMixer(obj);        // mixer.update(dt) in update()\nmixer.clipAction(clips.find(c => c.name === 'Walk')).play();\n```\n\nEvery critter has all seven joints, even if a snail's legs are empty groups, so every clip validates against every rig. Add extras (`Neck`, `Tail`) by turning them in code after `mixer.update`. Faces are small parts you scale or hide from code (blink, smile, talk). Hair and hats attach to the `Head` group.\n\n**Colours.** `model()` slots take only the 16 palette names. To use an off-palette accent, swap after the build: traverse, and where `mesh.material.color` matches a slot colour, assign `toonMaterial(0xf5a6c0)` [verified]. Allow at most four off-palette colours in the whole game (blossom pink is one). The same swap puts a pattern texture on a shirt: `toonMaterial(PALETTE.white, { map })`.\n\n**Library notes.**\n- `kenney-furniture` is near real size (bed 1.14×0.75×2.25 m, sofa 1.96×0.92×0.82 m); its `plant-small*` are tiny, so scale them. Slots: `wood metal metalLight metalDark carpet carpetDarker lamp plant`. Its defaults are nearly all orange and sand, so **recolour every set through `palette`**.\n- `kenney-nature` has `dirt grass stone` slots. Its orange trunks look wrong: remap them, or write your own trees (recommended, for seasons and fruit).\n\n### Surfaces\n\nThe ground is most of the screen and decides whether characters read. **The library's `meadow`, `flagstones` and `snow-drifts` are too noisy at this zoom** [verified by render]. Write quiet ones: a flat base plus sparse specks.\n\n```js\nconst full = c => ({ path: 'M0 0H32V32H0Z', fill: c });   // without a base layer the gaps render BLACK\nexport const lawn = textureDef({ id:'lawn', about:'Quiet lawn', tags:['surface'], size:[32,32], tile:true,\n  palette:{ a:'green', b:'lime' }, layers:[ full('a'), { pattern:'scatter', colors:['b'], count:9, size:1, seed:4 } ] });\nTEXTURE_LIBRARY.add(lawn);\n```\n\nRendered and approved: summer `green`+`lime`; spring `lime`+`green`+`white`; winter `white`+`mist`. **Autumn grass and paths must differ:** sand grass needs an orange path; winter paths are `mist`.\n\n### Occluders\n\nEvery frame, test the camera-to-player line against the bounding boxes of trees and buildings. Swap hits to shared dissolve materials (a small cache per colour, `.dissolve = 0.55`), and swap back with a little hysteresis. Don't clone per object.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Time, calendar, weather\n\n**Two clocks,** chosen on the first run and changeable in Settings:\n\n- **Village Time (default).** 1 game minute = 0.8 real seconds, so a full day takes about 19 minutes. The clock stops during dialogue, menus and shop screens. Day One runs at half speed until the errands are done.\n- **Real Clock.** The device's time and date, like the source. Season from month (Mar–May spring, Jun–Aug summer, Sep–Nov autumn, Dec–Feb winter); Sunday is Market Day, Saturday is Show Day, other days are Errand Days; the contest falls on the second Wednesday of the season's middle month, the festival on its last Saturday. Story beats follow days played, not dates. Readings arrive through `input.send`.\n\n**Calendar.** Four seasons of four days each, sixteen days a year: **Market Day, Errand Day, Show Day, Festival Day**. New day at 05:00. The HUD reads \"Spring · Market Day · 9:42 AM\". After Day 16 the year rolls over with the same calendar.\n\n**Bed** saves and offers: *Sleep till morning · Nap till noon · Nap till dusk · Hibernate until the next festival* (with a scolding from Mrs. Gander). Hibernate lets anyone reach every season without dev mode.\n\n**Season change** happens overnight behind a full-screen almanac page turning, with a title card and a sting. Reskin ground, canopies and props under cover (filter changes rebuild the pipeline once, so hide that too).\n\n| Season | Ground | Trees | Air |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Spring | lime, white specks | blossom pink, then lime | petals, showers, rainbow after rain |\n| Summer | green | deep green | fireflies, cicadas, thunder evenings (`screen.flash` + rumble) |\n| Autumn | sand, orange specks | orange, red, sand | blowing leaves, mushrooms, ground fog (`groundFog`) |\n| Winter | white, snow footprints (`Decals`) | bare or snow-capped | `Precipitation('snow')`, frosted windows, snowballs |\n\n**Weather** is seeded per day with an hourly start and stop: spring sun 60 / rain 30 / fog 10; summer sun 70 / rain 20 / storm 10; autumn sun 50 / wind 30 / rain 20; winter snow 60 / clear 40. Rain waters flowers, wakes snails, fills puddles, brings out umbrellas, and switches the music variant.\n\n**Day and night.** `applySky(engine, skyAt(hour))` each game minute. `skyAt(h).night` drives windows (glow parts on at 0.35), lamp lights (`ctx.lights`), fireflies, stars.\n\n**Absence** (both clocks, using a real timestamp kept in the save). After a real day or more away: +3 weeds per day (cap 40), 1–3 **dust bunnies** per day in your home (tiny, cowardly, net or stomp them, cap 8), mail piling up, and a first-talk line from each neighbour (\"Oh. You're alive. Dorrit owes me a pie.\"). Flowers wilt but don't die. Nothing is lost.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The village\n\nAn 80×64-tile map (1 tile = 1 m), five acres by four, each 16×16, authored as rows of characters in `src/data/townMap.js`. Tests flood-fill it: every door, stop and activity must be reachable. Fronts and doors face south or east, so the default camera sees them. North is cliff and waterfall; south is the sea. The map is original: don't reproduce any source town.\n\n| | Col 1 | Col 2 | Col 3 | Col 4 | Col 5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **A** (upper terrace, +2 m) | Pine wood | **Museum** | Waterfall and pool | Pear orchard, ramp | **Lookout** bench (stargazing) |\n| **B** | **Bus stop**, road out | Dorrit, Tavi, Nib; ramp up | River, north bridge | Bartleby, Ottoline; ramp up | Mossy wood, Rocco |\n| **C** | **Town Hall & Post** | **Plaza**: Old Pear tree, Old Bell, notice board, benches | River bend, south bridge, pond | **Mrs. Gander's store**, **Mothwick's** | **Your plot**, Ozzie, Yolanda |\n| **D** (beach) | Tide pools | Dock | River mouth | Lighthouse | Palm grove, driftwood |\n\n- **Hide the map's edges on all four camera turns:** a deep forest ring outside the walkable area on three sides, sea to the south.\n- **Terrain:** 16×16 chunks of merged meshes, one per surface, rebuilt when a tile changes. Cliff faces are textured boxes. One big `WaterSurface` under terrain with holes where water tiles are (`foamAt` high, so foam stays rare).\n- **Worn paths:** tiles walked often turn to path and heal slowly. The village shapes itself to you.\n- **Wind:** `swayObject` on canopies and grasses with one shared `WindUniforms`.\n- **Starting population:** about 55 trees, 6 rocks, flowers around homes, driftwood, shells, clothesline, chimneys that smoke.\n\n---\n\n## 5. You: avatar, movement, tools\n\n**Avatar.** A small round person of no particular species, and a running joke (\"What *are* you? No, don't tell me.\"). About 1.5 m to the crown, head about 55% of height: the proportions verified as readable at zoom 1.6. Eyes (6), hair (8) in colours (8), outfit, hat. Set by the bus conversation (section 13), then changed at the salon and mirror.\n\n**Movement.** Tiptoe 1.2 m/s (light tilt or Ctrl), walk 2.6, jog 4.2 (full tilt or plain WASD), run 6.0 (hold Circle/Shift). Running wears paths, spooks bugs and tramples flowers. Quick turns, squash on stopping, dust at speed. Pressing the tool button with empty hands gets a once-per-session gag: *\"You can't jump. You're in escrow.\"*\n\n**Targeting.** The target is the interactable within 1.2 m in a 70° cone, else the tile under `position + facing × 0.9`. A bouncing pixel chevron marks it; a chunky highlight marks the tile; a context prompt shows in the corner. With a mouse, hover drives all of this instead.\n\n**Tools** (equipped one at a time; L1/R1, wheel or 1–6):\n\n| Tool | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Net | Press to ready (holds pose, slows you), release to sweep: 1.8 m reach, 90° cone |\n| Rod | Cast at water; press again to reel (section 6) |\n| Shovel | Dig, refill, bury, hit rocks |\n| Axe | Three hits fell a tree; shovel removes the stump |\n| Watering can | Waters the target tile and its neighbours |\n| Slingshot | Shoots balloons; pad auto-aims the nearest ahead, mouse aims |\n\n**Bare-hand verbs:** talk, pick up, shake a tree, pull a weed, pick a flower, knock, sit, read, push a snowball. **Pockets:** 15 slots.\n\n**Reveal.** A first catch or find plays the hold-up pose, a jingle and its line (\"You caught a pike! Someone should tell it about the other end of the hook.\"). Repeats are quick.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Things to do\n\n**Fishing.** Shadows (S, M, L, XL) drift in water tiles (3 per water acre). Cast lands a bobber; a nearby shadow turns and comes. **1–3 false nibbles** (small dip, plip, faint rumble), then the **bite**: hard plunge, splash, \"!\", strong rumble. Reel window 0.6 s; miss and it flees. No other penalty.\n\n**Bugs.** About 1.5 per acre, by season, hour, weather and spot. Spook radius: run 3 m, jog 2, walk 1.2, tiptoe 0.5. Some sit on trunks, flowers, lamps or rocks. Hitting a rock can release a pill bug.\n\n| Fish (floor 14 → target 18) | Beans | Where | When | Line |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Minnow | 60 | river | not winter | \"That's… that's all of it.\" |\n| Perch | 150 | river | all, day | \"It was just sitting there. On purpose.\" |\n| Crawfish | 200 | pond | spring, summer | \"It has opinions about your thumbs.\" |\n| Carp | 300 | pond | all | \"Carp diem! …Sorry. It made me say that.\" |\n| Trout | 400 | river | spring, autumn | \"It isn't pouting. That's just its face.\" |\n| Catfish | 800 | pond | summer, autumn, night | \"It claims to be a different catfish online.\" |\n| Pike | 900 | river | autumn, winter | \"Someone should tell it about the other end of the hook.\" |\n| Eel | 1,200 | river | summer, autumn, dusk on | \"Slippery. Also, it knows what you did.\" |\n| Salmon | 1,500 | river mouth | autumn | \"It came all this way and now look.\" |\n| Golden Carp | 5,000 | pond | spring, dawn, rare | \"It looks expensive and it knows.\" |\n| Sturgeon | 6,000 | river mouth | winter, rare | \"Older than the Mayor. Slightly faster.\" |\n| Sardine | 100 | sea | all | \"Comes with its own sense of confinement.\" |\n| Mackerel | 250 | sea | summer, autumn | \"Holy it.\" |\n| Sea Bream | 500 | sea | spring, summer | \"Very calm. Suspiciously calm.\" |\n| Flounder | 700 | sea | all | \"Both eyes on you. Literally.\" |\n| Pufferfish | 1,800 | sea | summer, dusk on | \"It's taking this very personally.\" |\n| Moonfish | 4,000 | sea | summer, autumn, late, rare | \"It's bigger than your whole debt. Almost.\" |\n| Old Boot | 10 | anywhere | any (not for the museum) | \"Somewhere, a fish is walking funny.\" |\n\n| Bug (floor 14 → target 18) | Beans | Where | When | Line |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cabbage Butterfly | 90 | flowers | spring, summer, day | \"It's very sorry about the cabbage.\" |\n| Pill Bug | 100 | under rocks | all | \"It has rolled up and refused to comment.\" |\n| Ladybird | 120 | flowers | spring | \"It came for the aphids and stayed for the drama.\" |\n| Pond Skater | 130 | water | summer | \"Technically walking on water. Not technically impressed.\" |\n| Cricket | 130 | grass | autumn, night | \"It was just getting to the good part.\" |\n| Moth | 150 | lamps | all, night | \"It has found the lamp. It has found its purpose.\" |\n| Grasshopper | 160 | grass | summer, autumn | \"It didn't save anything for winter either.\" |\n| Dragonfly | 180 | near water | summer, autumn | \"Four wings and still somehow caught.\" |\n| Bumblebee | 200 | flowers | spring, summer | \"It's too busy for this, and it says so.\" |\n| Snail | 200 | rocks, rain only | spring, summer | \"Not the Mayor. Probably.\" |\n| Cicada | 250 | trunks | summer, day | \"Seventeen years underground for this.\" |\n| Swallowtail | 300 | flowers | summer, day | \"Fancy. It wants you to know that.\" |\n| Firefly | 300 | by water | summer, night | \"Dimmer up close. Aren't we all.\" |\n| Woolly Bear | 350 | trunks | autumn | \"Yolanda wants to know where it got that sweater.\" |\n| Praying Mantis | 400 | flowers | summer, autumn | \"It isn't praying. It's negotiating.\" |\n| Snow Flea | 600 | snow | winter, midday | \"Technically a bug. Emotionally, a snowflake.\" |\n| Stag Beetle | 2,000 | trunks | summer, night, rare | \"It has been waiting for someone to try that.\" |\n| Rhino Beetle | 2,500 | trunks | summer, late, rare | \"You're not sure who caught whom.\" |\n\n**Digging.** Three star-shaped cracks a day (`Decals` `crack`) hide fossils; a sparkle spot hides a bag of beans. Bags can be **buried and grown into a beanstalk** in 2 days, tripling them (\"Beans are currency, dear. You can't plant them.\" \"You can absolutely plant them. You didn't hear it from me.\"). One rock a day pays out when struck.\n\n**Trees.** Shake to drop what's in the canopy: pears (100 beans, regrow in 2 days), one tree a day has a bag or furniture, one has a wasp nest. Wasps chase you; a sting means a puffy-eye face until medicine or morning, and neighbours comment. Foreign fruit (500) comes from Gran's letters and can be planted. Saplings take 3 days, with a visual stage each day.\n\n**Flowers.** Four species, 3–5 colours. Plant, water, and hybrids appear beside touching same-species pairs that were watered (15% a night). Unwatered flowers wilt in 2 days. **Weeds:** 3 a day; pull by hand; neighbours notice.\n\n**Balloons.** Three a day drift over with a present on a string. Slingshot it; it falls somewhere awkward.\n\n**Beachcombing.** Six new shells a day, 15–300 beans, plus driftwood oddities.\n\n**Stargazing.** Sit on the Lookout bench at night: the camera glides up (`fixed` preset, `Timeline`) to a star field with six original constellations you can name in the Almanac (\"The Overdue Library Book\", \"The Big Spoon\"). Press on a **shooting star** to wish; a present arrives in the morning.\n\n**Snow.** In winter, push a snowball and it grows. Stack two (body, head) near each other: a snowman appears, comments on the proportions, and mails a gift if they were good.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Money\n\n**Beans** are the currency. Plenty of jokes: a hill of beans, spilling them, the bean counter.\n\n**Debt.** Mrs. Gander's terms, payable at the store, never enforced, never forgotten.\n\n| Home tier | Added debt | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 Starter shack | 8,000 | One 6×6 room |\n| 2 Proper cottage | 30,000 | One 8×8 room |\n| 3 Cottage with annex | 80,000 | Plus a 6×6 side room |\n| 4 Two storeys | 150,000 | Plus an 8×8 upstairs, and a plaque |\n\nFinishing tier 4 earns a gold-edged deed and Mrs. Gander's first compliment, which she immediately regrets. A diligent player clears tier 4 around Day 16 (expected income: 1–3k on Day 1, about 15k a day by midgame). Paint jobs (roof and wall colours) cost extra.\n\n**Store.** Mrs. Gander's, three tiers by lifetime spend (25k, 120k), with a new exterior each. Daily rotating stock is shown **as real objects on real shelves**: walk up, press to get price and yes/no. To sell, talk to her: your Pockets open and she quotes as you browse, no table. Stock: 2 furniture, 2 clothes, 1 wallpaper, 1 floor, tools, seeds, stationery, \"today's oddity\". Hours 8:00–22:00.\n\n**Cheese market** (our own speculative game). **Old Rind**, a whispering stoat, sells cheese wheels at the plaza on Market Day mornings (06:00–12:00) for 90–110 beans. Prices change at 08:00 and 14:00 on the next three days; pattern per season: Rising, Falling, Wobble or Spike (peak 3–6×), shown on the notice board. Wheels go bad at the next Market Day (then worth 1 bean, with a visible smell). Sell at the store.\n\n**Bean Jar** (Town Hall). Deposit and withdraw; 0.5% interest per season (\"We are very interested.\"). **Lost & Found** bin: leave things, find things. **Catalogue:** everything you've owned can be reordered at Post on big one-item cards; it arrives by mail next day. **Selling a gift a neighbour gave you** works, and they will hear about it.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Home\n\n- **Interior:** a dollhouse cutaway with two back walls, one iso camera, no turns [look verified]. Grid 1 m; items 1×1, 2×1, 2×2.\n- **Placement mode:** ✕ lifts or drops, L1/R1 rotates, mouse drags. Wallpaper and floor apply from Pockets. Items can't block the door.\n- **Interactive furniture (must work):** bed; chairs and sofas you sit on; lamps on/off (real pooled lights); **stereo** plays your collected tunes; **TV** shows a live feed of the plaza (`RenderView`, attached only while you're home); **mirror** really reflects (`Mirror`) and opens the wardrobe; fish tank and bug cage show what you put in; clock; toilet flushes pointlessly.\n- **Furniture:** floor 40 → target 60, in sets: Cottage, Mossy, Seaside, City Loft, Bureau, and the absurd Cheese set, plus singles. Most are `kenney-furniture` recoloured through `palette`; the signature pieces are your own models.\n- **Exterior:** one model per tier, mailbox with a flag up when there's mail.\n\n**Better Burrows Bureau rating** (section 11). Scored on Show Day nights, result by mail: points for item value, set bonuses (4+ matching), colour harmony, \"cozy rules\" (lamp by a seat, rug under a table, something growing), music playing, minus clutter. Grades C, B, A, S with a sentence of critique: *\"Too many chairs. Who are you expecting?\"* Reward furniture at each new grade.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Neighbours\n\n| Name | Species | Type | Tic | Home | Loves / hates |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Dorrit** | wombat | Hearth | \"…mmhm\" | Kitchen, too many pots | soup, rain / loud hats |\n| **Tavi** | quokka | Sunshine | \"isn't it?!\" | Everything yellow | everyone / nothing (it's worrying) |\n| **Bartleby** | moose | Grouch | \"I'd prefer not.\" | Bare room, one good chair; laundry stuck on his antlers | quiet, pine / being asked |\n| **Lady Ottoline** | platypus | Diva | \"darling dear\" | Faded grandeur, moth-eaten rug | pearls, flattery / mud (she lives in mud) |\n| **Rocco** | armadillo | Go-Getter | \"let's roll!\" | Gym, trophies he awarded himself | protein, fish / stairs |\n| **Nib** | lemur | Dreamer | \"…or so the moon says\" | Books, telescope, loose paper | stars, bugs / alarms |\n| **Ozzie** | possum | Schemer | \"You didn't hear it from me.\" | Corkboard and red string; plays dead when surprised | cheese, boxes / Mrs. Gander |\n| **Yolanda** | yak | Auntie | \"hon\" | Everything knitted, including the TV | gossip, wool / secrets she wasn't told |\n| **Fitch** (moves in, Day 9) | ferret | Hustler | \"no refunds\" | Half-unpacked boxes | deals / questions |\n\n**Relationships.** Tavi keeps waving at Bartleby. Ozzie suspects Mrs. Gander of everything. Ottoline and Yolanda are locked in a feud about a scarf. Dorrit feeds everyone. Rocco is training Nib against Nib's will. Lines reference these.\n\n**Routines.** Wake and sleep by type. Daily points of interest: plaza benches, riverbank (they fish, badly), store, museum, each other's doors. Rain: umbrellas or indoors. Night: home, window lit; knock after bedtime and get a sleepy complaint. Paths come from `NavGrid`. Two neighbours chat with bubbles; walk within 2 m to read a piece of it. They hum, sit, stare at bugs, carry things.\n\n**Friendship** 0–100 (stranger, acquaintance, friend, best friend). First talk of the day +3, favour +5–10, gift +2–10 by taste, good letter +3. Hitting someone with the net −2 (it does happen, they do comment).\n\n**Memory** is what sells it. Keep `memory` events: gifts, catches, sales, outfits, net bonks, absences. Lines pull from them: *\"Heard you sold the scarf Yolanda knitted you. To the goose. She's not mad. She's knitting about it.\"*\n\n**Favours.** Two a day, marked with a \"!\" over their head: fetch a named item, deliver a parcel, catch something within an hour, return a lost thing, trade. Floor 12 → target 20 templates, flavoured per type.\n\n**Letters.** Mail arrives at 09:00: neighbour letters (with presents, per friendship), Gran's passive-aggressive note each season with a foreign fruit or something inexplicable, museum thanks, Bureau results, catalogue orders, the occasional anonymous tip from Ozzie. You write letters at Post, on paper, with an attached gift, using the on-screen keyboard or typing. Replies next day react to what you wrote: their name present, length, gift. *\"You wrote 'asdfgh'. I framed it.\"*\n\n**Move-out and move-in.** A complete bittersweet beat: Rocco talks about leaving on Day 7 (you may argue, and it changes his goodbye), boxes appear, he's gone on Day 9 with a goodbye letter and, if a best friend, his favourite trophy. Fitch moves into his house that same morning. Rocco writes later and rolls back to visit on the last night. Dev mode can trigger any move.\n","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":925}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019drUbExmgropzoQXR5QEZ3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
