{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkiQoB4L8HnJqgjEQUf4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018xRE7fYKdTh7veKUQEhyez","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/PLAN.md","offset":400,"limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21322,"cache_read_input_tokens":19365,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21322},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7fc0c99-6050-4a36-bbad-13de512b63e9","uuid":"23be9a39-a8d9-435c-9066-5a9ae9ff7706","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:18:30.138Z","request_id":"req_011CgkiQnnjtrki4fWWZQa7t","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018xRE7fYKdTh7veKUQEhyez":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/PLAN.md","offset":400,"limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018xRE7fYKdTh7veKUQEhyez","type":"tool_result","content":"400\t\n401\t---\n402\t\n403\t## 10. Town services\n404\t\n405\t| Place | Who | What |\n406\t|---|---|---|\n407\t| **Store** | Mrs. Gander (goose, spectacles, waistcoat, ledger) | Section 7 |\n408\t| **Town Hall & Post** | Clementine (capybara, unflappable, a bee lives on her head and she consults it) | Bean Jar, Lost & Found, mail, catalogue, town rating, **Village Chime** editor |\n409\t| **Plaza** | Mayor Trundle (snail with sash) | He has been crossing the plaza to cut a ribbon since before you came, and visibly advances each day. He arrives on Festival Day of spring. |\n410\t| **Museum** | Dr. Quill (near-sighted pangolin who rolls into a ball when overjoyed) | Section below |\n411\t| **Mothwick's** | Madame Mothwick (dramatic moth, nibbles hems when nervous) | Clothes, hats, **pattern studio** |\n412\t\n413\t**Museum.** A hall and four wings you walk through, each donation appearing at once in place:\n414\t\n415\t- **Aquarium** with real tanks and swimming fish, **Insectarium** with live flutter.\n416\t- **Fossil Hall:** two original composite skeletons (Moatosaur: skull, torso, tail; Long-Necked Regretodon: skull, neck, torso) plus singles (trilobite, ammonite, amber fly, mystery tooth): 10 pieces.\n417\t- **Gallery:** 8 parody paintings of public-domain works: *Mona Llama, Goose with a Pearl Earring, The Great Wave Off Beans, The Starry Bus Stop, The Birth of Venus Flytrap, The Honk, Whistler's Moose, The Cheese of Earthly Delights.*\n418\t\n419\tDr. Quill misidentifies everything first (\"A fossil! Or a very confident potato!\") and appraises unidentified fossils. Filling a wing earns a plaque with your name, misspelled.\n420\t\n421\t**Village Chime.** Sixteen steps on a chunky note pad, played on the Old Bell every hour, at doors, and at the finale. A player's first act of authorship; the Hall asks for it on Day 3. Also used as the store door jingle.\n422\t\n423\t**Clothes and patterns.**\n424\t- Clothes floor 20 → target 30 (palette plus simple textures), hats 8 → 12, glasses 3 → 6, umbrellas 3 → 6.\n425\t- **Pattern studio:** a 16×16 pixel editor with the 16 palette colours, 8 save slots, pen, fill, mirror, undo, playable by pad or mouse with a big grid and live preview on your avatar.\n426\t- Patterns go on shirts, umbrellas, signs and ground tiles, and on your **festival firework**. Stored as texture data, shown with `DataTexture` + `pixelTexture` + `toonMaterial(white, { map })`.\n427\t\n428\t**Town rating.** Clementine gives one honest sentence and one hint (trees 40–70, flowers, weeds, litter). A high rating on the last night turns the Old Bell gold.\n429\t\n430\t---\n431\t\n432\t## 11. The City\n433\t\n434\t**The bus.** Press ✕ at the stop; it arrives in seconds, and the ride is a 20-second skippable scene (the bus rocking through a seasonal countryside diorama). **Hester**, a llama driver, sings a ballad about your recent life in gibberish with subtitles, made from memory events: *\"♪ Oh, you fished up an old boot and you sold it for ten, / and you'd do it, you'd do it, you'd do it again… ♪\"* Floor 20 verse templates, plus choruses. The fare is listening.\n435\t\n436\t**The place.** An L-shaped boulevard around a plaza, about 40×24 tiles, in its own Group. **Day:** bright, busy. **Night:** the signature look: dark street, bright glass, neon. Use `glow` parts, real colored pooled lights at each front, and a whole-scene `bloom` filter. **Bloom's default strength blows out characters and glass; `{ id: 'bloom', params: { amount: 0.3 } }` in the look's `scene` stack keeps them readable** [verified by render]. City nights only, swapped under a transition. Signs are 3D: letters built from your bitmap font as small `glow` boxes (about 0.08 m per pixel), so they read as neon.\n437\t\n438\t**Crowd.** 12–18 pedestrians of assorted species built from the critter generator, walking the boulevard with short overheard lines; a street musician; pigeons (`Boids`) that scatter when you run. Crowd murmur is low-volume gibberish.\n439\t\n440\t**Venues** (each one room, each with its own music and at least one interaction that isn't a menu; open from your first visit, Magda's only after dark):\n441\t\n442\t| Venue | Host | What makes it fun |\n443\t|---|---|---|\n444\t| **Snip & Gossip** (salon) | Monsieur Fleece, an alpaca with magnificent hair | Live preview of hair and colour on your avatar on a turntable; he tells you one piece of gossip per cut |\n445\t| **Maison Plume** (couture) | Flavia Rosé, a flamingo behind a velvet rope | **Fashion check:** a daily theme (\"Something damp\"); your outfit's tags are judged aloud, 0–5 plumes; plumes unlock the couture rail |\n446\t| **Magda's Genuine Articles** (after dark, down an alley) | Magda, a magpie | Three paintings a visit at a premium, some forged. Compare with the catalogue print (a missing star, an extra earring). Forgeries get a very disappointed Dr. Quill. Rare furniture too. \"One hundred percent genuine, and only forty percent stolen.\" |\n447\t| **The Palace Theatre** (evenings) | Sir Reginald Bellows, a hippo thespian | A short sketch per visit; press Laugh on the punchline to learn an **emote** (8 in all: laugh, cry, shock, anger, love, shrug, bow, dance) |\n448\t| **Pemberton's Auction Rooms** (10:00–18:00) | Pemberton, a dormouse who speaks at double speed | Three lots. Raise your paddle against three AI bidders with hidden limits and a \"going once…\" clock. You can also consign an item; the result arrives by mail |\n449\t| **Better Burrows Bureau** | Agent Brisket, a badger | Explains home scoring, critiques your home in person, gives grade rewards |\n450\t| **The Daily Grind** (café) | Languid Lou, a sloth who barely speaks | Three blends: a coffee gives a jog-speed buff until noon, and Lou's three words of the day hint at a rare catch |\n451\t| **Madame Nibbles' tent** (plaza) | a goat who eats her tarot cards mid-reading | A daily fortune with half of it chewed off: *\"I see a tall stranger. He's holding… (chew) …that part's gone now. Good luck, though.\"* It nudges luck (fish, finds, friendship) until morning |\n452\t\n453\t---\n454\t\n455\t## 12. The year\n456\t\n457\t| Day | Season, type | What happens |\n458\t|---|---|---|\n459\t| 1 | Spring, Market | Arrival, errands, tools (section 13) |\n460\t| 2 | Spring, Errand | Museum opens with your fossil; Axe and Slingshot go on sale at the store; first City trip |\n461\t| 3 | Spring, Show | Compose the Chime; Vesper plays at 20:00 |\n462\t| 4 | Spring, **Festival** | **The Seed Toss**; the Mayor arrives |\n463\t| 5 | Summer, Market | Season turn; Old Rind's cheese |\n464\t| 6 | Summer, Errand | **Fishing Derby** |\n465\t| 7 | Summer, Show | Rocco talks about leaving; Vesper |\n466\t| 8 | Summer, **Festival** | **The Lantern Float** |\n467\t| 9 | Autumn, Market | Rocco leaves; Fitch moves in |\n468\t| 10 | Autumn, Errand | **Bug-Off**; mushrooms in the woods |\n469\t| 11 | Autumn, Show | Vesper; soup ingredients start to matter |\n470\t| 12 | Autumn, **Festival** | **The Great Soup** |\n471\t| 13 | Winter, Market | First snow |\n472\t| 14 | Winter, Errand | **Snowman Contest** |\n473\t| 15 | Winter, Show | A letter from Rocco; Vesper |\n474\t| 16 | Winter, **Festival** | **The Long Night**, year-end, credits |\n475\t\n476\t**Show nights.** **Vesper**, a fruit bat, plays tiny accordion hanging upside down from a plaza lamp post (\"That's how the notes fall out.\"). Ask for a mood; she plays one of 6 songs, sings in gibberish, and gives you the record for your stereo. Neighbours gather and sway.\n477\t\n478\t**Festivals** (each a full-village set piece with decorations and its own music):\n479\t\n480\t- **The Seed Toss (spring).** Everyone flings seeds at chalked rings for 90 seconds. Flowers burst from every hit; the town blooms the next morning in the pattern you sowed. The Mayor finally reaches the ribbon, and it's gone.\n481\t- **The Lantern Float (summer).** At dusk, pick a lantern colour and a one-line wish, set it on the river at the bridge, and watch hundreds of lights drift to the sea. Fireworks over the beach at 21:00, **including one in your own pattern**. Your wish gets a reply by letter.\n482\t- **The Great Soup (autumn).** Dorrit's giant pot on the plaza. Add three ingredients: fish, fruit, mushroom, boot. It's rated from their tags, with neighbours tasting aloud. Good soup means a feast and a reward; bad soup means a village of queasy faces and a gag-gift next day.\n483\t- **The Long Night (winter).** The sun sets at 16:00. Carry a string of lights to each door, exchange a gift, then gather at the Old Pear at midnight as it lights up and the Bell plays your Chime, harmonised. Rocco rolls in. Clementine reads the town rating, and at a high rating the Bell turns gold. An almanac of your year (biggest catch, most-sold-to, debt status, who you talked to most) leads into credits. Year Two begins next morning with a letter.\n484\t\n485\t**Contests** (timed 3 game hours; neighbours post real scores from the same rules; trophy furniture for placing): Fishing Derby, biggest fish; Bug-Off, most value; Snowman Contest, best proportions, judged by Bartleby, who would prefer not.\n486\t\n487\t---\n488\t\n489\t## 13. Day One\n490\t\n491\tThe first 20 minutes teach by errand. Clock at half speed until done.\n492\t\n493\t1. **Title → New Arrival.** Choose Village Time or Real Clock in one line each.\n494\t2. **The bus.** Night, rain, Hester. \"End of the line? Nobody gets off there on purpose.\" She asks your name, what the town's called (\"I only drive the bus\"), then: *What are you running from? Favourite weather? How do you take bad news?* Answers set hair, colours and eyes. A reflection in the bus window lets you adjust the look. \"You look like someone who owes money.\"\n495\t3. **The stop.** Mrs. Gander, ledger open: \"You're late. No, you're exactly on time. I've already billed you for being late.\" She walks you to a roofless shack. \"The roof is included. The rain is complimentary. The 8,000 beans are not.\" Apron on: you work it off.\n496\t4. **Errand list** (a short HUD list; each takes about 2 minutes and pays off part of the debt):\n497\t   - Fetch a parcel from Dorrit (talk, map, soup joke).\n498\t   - Pull weeds, sell one at the store (pick up, sell).\n499\t   - Tavi gives you a **Net**: catch a butterfly (sneak, swing, reveal).\n500\t   - Bartleby has a **Rod** and would prefer not: catch him a fish anyway (cast, bite).\n501\t   - Mayor Trundle lends a **Shovel**, already on his way somewhere: dig the crack on the plaza (dig, fossil).\n502\t   - **Watering can** from Clementine: water the Mayor's sapling.\n503\t   - Write to Gran at Post (letter UI, on-screen keyboard).\n504\t5. **Evening.** \"You've paid off 2,000 beans. You're free to go. Not that you were ever not free. Legally.\" Dusk, lit windows, first hourly chime. Bed saves. Gran's reply and a neighbour's letter are in the mailbox next morning.\n505\t\n506\tEvery errand demonstrates a verb. Wandering off is fine: the list waits.\n507\t\n508\t---\n509\t\n510\t## 14. Writing bible\n511\t\n512\t**Voice.** Warm, deadpan, gently absurd. Everyone is a bit rude and it's affectionate. Short lines. Jokes land at the end of the sentence. Sincerity is allowed, rarely, and hits harder for it. Capitalism is a fond joke. No irony about the player's feelings. British-or-American spelling: pick one and hold it.\n513\t\n514\t**Samples to match:**\n515\t\n516\t- **Mrs. Gander:** \"No rush on the payment, dear. I've simply noted the time you didn't pay, and I'll be noting it again tomorrow.\" / \"HONK. Pardon me. That was my indoor voice.\"\n517\t- **Mayor Trundle:** \"I declare this… ribbon… cut. Has anyone… seen… the ribbon? It was here… in March.\"\n518\t- **Clementine:** \"Your letter's stamped. A bee has been living in the stamp drawer. We've agreed to share.\"\n519\t- **Dr. Quill:** \"Is it a fossil? It's a fossil! Or a very confident potato! Either way, it goes in the hall!\"\n520\t- **Dorrit:** \"You look peaky, mmhm. I made soup. It's Tuesday's. Tuesday was a good day for soup, mmhm.\"\n521\t- **Tavi:** \"I smiled at a rock today and I think it's going really well, isn't it?!\"\n522\t- **Bartleby:** \"You're standing in my good spot. No, there isn't another one. They're all my good spot. I'd prefer not.\"\n523\t- **Lady Ottoline:** \"I am technically the Duchess of this river, darling dear. Yes, that one. No, don't touch it.\"\n524\t- **Rocco:** \"I rolled here! From over there! That's thirty metres of pure cardio, let's roll!\"\n525\t- **Nib:** \"I wrote you a poem. It's about your hat. It ends badly, or so the moon says.\"\n526\t- **Ozzie:** \"Have you ever *seen* Mrs. Gander eat? No. Neither has anyone. You didn't hear it from me.\"\n527\t- **Yolanda:** \"Hon, I knitted you a thing. Not sure what it is yet. Give it time, hon.\"\n528\t- **Inspector Pimm:** \"Pimm. Department of Continuity. You left. No bed. No goodbye. Do you know what that does to a Tuesday?\"\n529\t- **Vesper:** \"Sit anywhere, friend. I'd offer you a chair, but I'm using the ceiling.\"\n530\t- **Flavia Rosé:** \"Darling. Stand in better lighting. No. Further. Keep going.\"\n531\t- **Old Rind:** \"Psst. Wheel of cheese? It only goes up. Mostly. Until it goes off.\"\n532\t\n533\t**Volumes** (floor → target, total about 700 → 1,200 lines):\n534\t\n535\t| Bank | Lines |\n536\t|---|---|\n537\t| Per type: greetings, weather, time of day, season, absent, hungry, gift reactions, favours, letters | 40 → 80 each (8 types) |\n538\t| Unique per neighbour | 12 → 30 each |\n539\t| Gossip templates | 30 → 60 |\n540\t| Mrs. Gander | 30 |\n541\t| Each other named host | 15–30 |\n542\t\n543\t**Mechanics.** Lines are data: `{ id, text, when: { season, weather, hour, friendMin, memory }, weight }`, with tags `{player} {town} {item} {neighbour} {catch}`, `|` for a beat, `*word*` for emphasis, and name colours. Choices are 2–4 options. Each line should be seen once before it repeats. Neighbours speak with their tic often but not always.\n544\t\n545\t---\n546\t\n547\t## 15. UI and UX\n548\t\n549\tController first, on a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse get the same screens and should feel native.\n550\t\n551\t| Action | DualSense (Xbox glyph) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n552\t|---|---|---|---|\n553\t| Move | Left stick: tilt sets tiptoe, walk, jog | WASD or arrows | Left-click ground to walk there; hold to follow the cursor |\n554\t| Run | Hold ○ (B) | Hold Shift | |\n555\t| Sneak | Light tilt | Hold Ctrl | |\n556\t| Talk, open, pick up, shake, confirm | ✕ (A) | E or Enter | Left-click the thing (walks into reach first) |\n557\t| Use tool | □ (X) | Space or F | Right-click where you're aiming |\n558\t| Back | ○ (B) | Esc or Backspace | Right-click in menus |\n559\t| Pockets | △ (Y) | Tab or I | Click its icon |\n560\t| Previous, next tool | L1, R1 (LB, RB) | 1–6 | Wheel |\n561\t| Turn camera ±90° | L2, R2 (LT, RT) or right-stick flick | Q, R | |\n562\t| Zoom step | Right-stick up, down | + − | |\n563\t| Map | Touchpad press or D-pad up | M | Click |\n564\t| Almanac | D-pad down | J | Click |\n565\t| Emotes | D-pad left | G | Click |\n566\t| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc | |\n567\t\n568\tCreate stays unused: **Create + Options belongs to the Hub.** Rumble via `vibrationActuator` (bite, dig, thunder) where available.\n569\t\n570\t**Prompts** follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs for controllers (✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1 L2 R2 Options, drawn as pixel sprites), Xbox glyphs (A B X Y LB RB LT RT Menu) with a Settings option, keycaps and mouse-button icons for keyboard and mouse. Corner prompt shows at most three. **Mouse:** a pixel cursor (OS cursor hidden); hover moves focus in menus and highlights things in the world with a name tooltip; click activates.\n571\t\n572\t**HUD.** Date and time top left, beans top right, tool bottom left, context prompt bottom right. It fades after a few idle seconds and returns on change. Safe margins: 12 art pixels on all sides.\n573\t\n574\t**Dialogue.** Bottom panel, three lines, nameplate, typewriter with voice, skip to end then advance on ✕. The camera eases in a little (`setCamera` tween to zoom 2.0). The speaker faces you.\n575\t\n576\t**Screens** (each is a focused single idea; **never a table**, never tiny text, no scrollbars, pages instead, at most 8 focusable things except the grids below):\n577\t\n578\t| Screen | Form |\n579\t|---|---|\n580\t| Pockets | 5×3 big cells (art ×2), selected item's name and one sentence, actions on press |\n581\t| Tool ring | Pops around you while cycling |\n582\t| Map | Pixel map, pins, who's where now |\n583\t| Almanac | Tabs: Fish, Bugs, Fossils, Art, Stars, Calendar; big silhouettes that fill in |\n584\t| Letter | A sheet of paper with big type; on-screen keyboard grid (pad), typing (keyboard) |\n585\t| Name entry | Same keyboard, plus △ for a random suggestion |\n586\t| Catalogue | One big item card at a time, by category |\n587\t| Placement | In-room grid, nothing else on screen |\n588\t| Pattern studio | Big 16×16 grid, palette strip, preview |\n589\t| Chime | 16 tall chunky columns |\n590\t| Emote wheel | Eight wedges |\n591\t\n592\t**Icons.** About 45 category sprites (16×16 rows, recoloured per item: 4 fish shapes, 5 bug shapes, chair, lamp, shirt…). Don't try to author one per item.\n593\t\n594\t**Touch-only devices** (`touchOnly` above, no pad connected): **no touch controls.** The title shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button (⚙). The attract mode still runs after 10 seconds; a tap exits it. A key press or pad press clears the notice and the game plays normally.\n595\t\n596\t**The engine's ⚙ panel's Reset sets music back to 60%** (its default). On touch-only devices, where the panel is reachable, wrap `audio.resetVolumes` on the instance so it restores 10%. Elsewhere, the game's own Settings replaces the panel.\n597\t\n598\t---\n599\t\n600\t## 16. Art direction\n601\t\n602\t**Base style:** the engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art with the default look (`LOOK_PRESETS.none`): toon bands, pixel outlines, Sweetie 16. Rendered comparisons: `none` and `pixel_world` read best, `dream` blooms too much, `cel_cartoon` stops feeling like pixel art. Use `none`.\n603\t\n604\t**Readability rules.** Quiet ground, loud actors. Dark outlines. Chunky shapes (≥ 0.25 m, per the guide). Doors, windows and signs are the most saturated things on a building. Reference proportions [verified]: cottage walls 4.4×2.2×3.6 m with two wedge roofs and a chimney; critters 1.5 m with a 0.9 m head; `lamp-post`, `notice-board`, `park-bench`, `market-stall`, `fountain` from the library work as they are.\n605\t\n606\t**New assets to make (they're what makes it feel like this game):**\n607\t\n608\t- **Critter generator** (`src/art/critter.js`): parts for head shape, ears, antlers, bills, tails, shell, hair, clothes, from one function. All neighbours, hosts and crowd come from it. Each needs a distinct silhouette at 45 px: moose antlers with laundry, platypus bill and tiara, armadillo bands, lemur ringed tail (`SpringChain`), yak hair curtain, snail shell and sash, goose neck and spectacles, bat hanging, hermit crab in a filing cabinet, flamingo legs, alpaca hair.\n609\t- **Buildings:** home ×4 tiers, store ×3, Town Hall with Post flag, Museum with columns, Mothwick's with giant spool, 9 cottages (shared shell, personal decoration), bus and shelter, 8 City fronts with neon, City skyline, lighthouse.\n610\t- **Nature:** own trees (pear, pine, palm, plus stages) with seasonal palettes and fruit, flowers (4 species), weeds, saplings, beanstalk, Old Pear (huge), Old Bell, river banks, cliffs, ramps, bridges.\n611\t- **Items:** tools; fish and bugs (parametric: body, fins, colour; library `butterfly`/`firefly` sprites are fine for flyers); fossils; 8 paintings (pixel `TextureDef`s with a forgery variant); hats; furniture signature pieces; cheese wheel; snowman; lanterns; balloon.\n612\t\n613\t**Life everywhere.** Blinking, tails, smoke, swaying crowns, cloud shadows, birds (`Boids`), fish rings, laundry, butterflies, puddles, petals, leaves, lit windows. If a screen is ever still for 3 seconds, something's missing. Performance is out of scope: add what the scene needs.\n614\t\n615\t**Every sheet goes through** `node check.mjs --sheets` from your kit (exit 0, no problems; warnings fixed unless you mean them). **Look at the PNGs in the game too.**\n616\t\n617\t---\n618\t\n619\t## 17. Animation\n620\t\n621\t**System:** the engine's clip format (`ClipDef` data at 30 fps, full poses, easing, sine layers), compiled with `compileClips` onto the 7-joint rig, played with `THREE.AnimationMixer`, cross-faded with `crossFadeTo`. Plus `Squash` (stop, land, pick up), `SpringChain` (tails, ears, ribbons), code-driven face parts, and head turns applied after the mixer.\n622\t\n623\t**Clips (floor 30):** Idle, IdleBored, Walk, Jog, Run, Tiptoe, Talk, Listen, Nod, ShakeHead, Laugh, Cry, Shock, Anger, Love, Shrug, Bow, Dance, Wave, Sit, Sleep, PlayDead, HoldUp, PickUp, Give, NetReady, NetSweep, Cast, Reel, Dig, Chop, Water, ShakeTree, Slingshot, PushSnow, Eat, Hum, Sway, Sting, Slip, plus species specials: Mayor's slide, Vesper hanging, Pimm's sideways scuttle, Rocco's roll.\n624\t\n625\t**Feel notes.** Net: 3 frames of anticipation then a fast arc. Tree shake: canopy wobble and leaves. Dig: three dirt chunks, then a hole. Bite: bobber snaps under. HoldUp: both arms, item above head, pause. Dialogue: small nods and talk mouth on syllables. `validateClip(clip, RIG)` returns nothing in tests; check `renderSheet` contact sheets for a few key clips.\n626\t\n627\t---\n628\t\n629\t## 18. Audio\n630\t\n631\tEverything is code: no audio files. Music plays through the engine's `Song` sequencer, effects through `SoundDef`s.\n632\t\n633\t**Music volume defaults to 10%.** The engine's default is 60%, stored under `pixel-engine:audio`. On a profile with no stored audio, call `audio.setVolume('music', 0.1)` before any music starts. **Author tracks hot**: make simultaneous track volumes sum to about 1.0 [verified numbers, section 2], or 10% is inaudible. Settings Reset restores 10%. Test it.\n634\t\n635\t**Music** (all new; easygoing, a little jazzy, lots of walking bass; `stepsPerBeat: 3` gives a swing feel):\n636\t\n637\t| Set | Count (floor → target) |\n638\t|---|---|\n639\t| **Hourly tunes:** every one of the 24 hours has its own arrangement, each with rain and snow variants, built by `composer.js` from hand-written motifs (transposition, tempo, re-voicing) | 8 → 12 motifs |\n640\t| Title, credits, bus ride, bed lullaby | 4 |\n641\t| Venues: store, museum, Mothwick's, Hall, City day, City night, salon, couture, Magda's, theatre, auction, café, Bureau | 13 |\n642\t| Festivals, contests, season stings | 4 + 1 + 4 |\n643\t| Vesper's records | 6 |\n644\t\n645\tOn the hour: Bell plays the Chime, then the new tune starts. Indoors, the venue's tune replaces it. At home, your stereo. `parseSong` on every song in tests.\n646\t\n647\t**Ambience** with `audio.loop` voices and `spatialize`: river, surf, rain, wind, crickets, birds, City hum, neon buzz.\n648\t\n649\t**Effects** (floor 40): footsteps per surface, each tool, splashes, bite, pick-ups, reveal jingle, UI tick, confirm, cancel, doors, mail flag, bus engine, HONK, paddle, cheese smell, firework.\n650\t\n651\t**Voices: gibberish, Animalese/Simlish style, code only** [method verified]. Each letter of the typed line triggers a very short syllable: a `SoundDef` of stacked sine harmonics whose weights follow vowel formants (a harmonic stack through `layers`), with noise layers for consonants.\n652\t\n653\t```js\n654\tfunction vowel(f0, f1, f2, f3, dur = 0.09) {            // renderSound: peak about 0.8, 5 ms to render\n655\t  const amp = h => { const f = f0*h, g = (c,w,a) => a*Math.exp(-((f-c)**2)/(2*w*w)); return g(f1,90,1)+g(f2,120,.6)+g(f3,180,.25); };\n656\t  const layers = []; for (let h = 2; h*f0 < 4000; h++) { const v = amp(h)*.45; if (v > .01) layers.push({ wave:'sine', freq:f0*h, attack:.008, sustain:dur*.55, decay:dur*.45, volume:v }); }\n657\t  return { wave:'sine', freq:f0, attack:.008, sustain:dur*.55, decay:dur*.45, volume:Math.max(.08, amp(1)*.45), vibrato:{ depth:.2, rate:18 }, layers };\n658\t}\n659\t```\n660\t\n661\tRegister about 10 syllables per voice archetype (6 archetypes); each character has a base pitch (`play(name, { pitch })`), speed and lilt; question marks rise; capitals are loud; Mrs. Gander's HONK is a square sweep; Pemberton plays at double speed; Lou is low and slow. Singing (Hester, Vesper) uses the same syllables at melody pitches. A separate **Voices** slider scales `volume` per play (the engine has only master, sfx, music channels). Settings offers Gibberish, Soft beeps or Off. Audio unlocks on first input, so attract is silent until then; `audio.counts` still counts, which tests use.\n662\t\n663\t---\n664\t\n665\t## 19. Multiplayer\n666\t\n667\tFollow `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md` (AI Hub Session Protocol v1); `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` sit beside it. Two modes share one host/guest code path behind a `Transport` interface: `send(data, { to, reliable })`, `on('message')`, `players`.\n668\t\n669\t### In an AI Hub session: shared world, \"The Bean Bazaar\"\n670\t\n671\tWhen `AIHub.active`, the game opens straight into the round: no title, attract, menus, pause, dev mode, saves or quit. Build only the arena. **Call `AIHub.connect` first thing, before `Engine.start`** (hello times out at 10 s). Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the arena is built (the harness allows 20 s with four software-rendered copies, so aim under 8 s) with no key press. If `connect()` answers `null`, there's no hub after all: carry on with the normal start.\n672\t\n673\t```html\n674\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n675\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n676\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n677\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"5\">\n678\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Bank the most beans before the last bell\">\n679\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move: stick or WASD. Do the thing: Cross or E. Sell at the stall.\">\n680\t<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">\n681\t```\n682\t\n683\t**The round.** Five minutes on a 32×24 Fair Green (pond and river strip, small orchard, flower patch, dig cracks, a central stall), in the village's art.\n684\t\n685\t- All verbs are contextual on one button, no tool switching: shake, dig, cast, reel, swing, sell.\n686\t- Five pocket slots. Unbanked value counts half at the bell, so trips to the stall matter.\n687\t- Neighbours post short orders (\"Dorrit wants a pear: double beans\").\n688\t- A bell announces golden moments (golden fish, a rich tree) at set times. The Mayor inches across the green; bump him for a tip of the sash and small bonus.\n689\t- Avatars wear seat colours (`#e8443a #3b82f6 #22c55e #f5b700`) with hub names over their heads (never inserted as HTML). A scoreboard strip shows everyone.\n690\t\n691\t**Own card** (`connect({ mode:'shared-world', seats:[1,4], card:true })`): the plaza, players lined up in their colours, the game's name, who's ready, how to win, this player's controls, time left from the `card` event. Answers: Ready = Enter, Cross or click, `AIHub.answer('ready')`; More time = Triangle, a named key or click, `AIHub.answer('more')`. Nothing else on the card; it changes nothing. Read Enter with a plain `keydown` listener on `window`: the harness pokes a synthetic key and allows 3 s, and software-rendered frames can be slow.\n692\t\n693\t**Protocol duties.**\n694\t- Play starts at `AIHub.time() >= 0`; show the 3-second countdown from `start`.\n695\t- Host is authoritative. Bell at min(5:00, `limitMs` − 5 s): `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })` listing every player still in `AIHub.players`, ties grouped. Comic awards (\"Most Fish Apologised To\").\n696\t- On `time-up`, finish at once.\n697\t- On `leave`, remove the avatar.\n698\t- On `end`, stop input, `audio.stopMusic()` and `stopLoops()`, send nothing.\n699\t- `server` copy: `AIHub.server` true, no local player, input or avatar; slow overview camera; runs the world as usual.\n700\t- Use `AIHub.rng(label)` for host-independent randomness.\n701\t\n702\t**Session rules to hold.** Network only through the hub. **No RTC or BroadcastChannel objects are even created in a session** (the check watches for sockets): load those modules only when `!AIHub.active`. No chat, emotes, typed names or `alert`/`confirm`/`prompt`/`window.open`. Ignore Options while Create is held. A touch-only player without a pad stays connected as a spectator with the notice on screen.\n703\t\n704\t### On its own: Visit\n705\t\n706\tFrom the title: **Open my gate** or **Visit a friend**. A guest arrives by bus in the host's town. Same avatars, tools and neighbours; the host's world is authoritative.\n707\t\n708\t| Transport | Use | Note |\n709\t|---|---|---|\n710\t| **BroadcastChannel** | Two windows on one computer, no typing | Hosts announce on a channel; guests see them listed. Same browser profile only [verified]. |\n711\t| **WebRTC with invite codes** | Two computers, no server | A **Gate Pass** (offer) goes to the friend, who pastes it and returns a **Reply Slip** (answer). `CompressionStream('deflate-raw')` + base64 gets about 600 characters; wait up to 4 s for ICE gathering [verified]. Use a public STUN server for the internet; it still works on a LAN if offline. Paste through a pixel-styled DOM textarea (needs a keyboard, and says so); Copy with a selected-text fallback. A knowing joke about friend codes is welcome. |\n712\t\n713\tUp to 3 guests in a star. On join the host sends a chunked reliable town snapshot (tiles, objects, weather, time, neighbours) so the guest builds a temporary town; guests' own save holds only their pockets and beans, written on leaving. Guests may catch, pick fruit, chat through emotes and canned phrases, drop gifts; they can't fell trees, pull flowers or use the bus (\"Bus doesn't run while you have company.\"). Host leaving sends guests home.\n714\t\n715\t### Netcode (both modes)\n716\t\n717\t| Message | Direction | Rate |\n718\t|---|---|---|\n719\t| `me`: x, z, facing, action | guest → host, unreliable | 10 Hz |\n720\t| `do`: action request (catch, pick, sell…) | guest → host, reliable | on action |\n721\t| `snap`: all avatars, moving objects (≤ 1,100 bytes) | host → all, unreliable | 10 Hz |\n722\t| `ev`: results, spawns, score, bell | host → all, reliable | on change |\n723\t| `town`: chunks ≤ 12 KB | host → guest, reliable | once, Visit only |\n724\t\n725\tKeep the Hub's budget everywhere (≤ 20 messages a second, burst 40, ≤ 16 KB): `Transport` enforces it in Visit too, so one code path passes the harness. Own avatar is client-predicted; remote avatars render 120 ms in the past and snap if more than 3 m off. Catches are claimed by the client and validated by the host (still there, in reach). Tested at `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`.\n726\t\n727\t---\n728\t\n729\t## 20. Title, attract, pause, settings, dev mode, About, saves\n730\t\n731\t**Title.** The live village diorama with the logo (bitmap font at ×3–4). Menu: **Continue · New Arrival · Visit · Settings · About**. Prompts per device.\n732\t\n733\t**Attract mode.** After **10 seconds with no input on the title/menu screen** (not inside Settings, About or name entry), a looping AI-played demo starts; **any input** (key, button, stick, mouse move, tap) exits to the title at once. It runs on a throwaway demo save and never touches the player's. It cheats freely (teleports, time and season jumps behind quick irises, no clip, infinite beans). Its only job is to show as much as possible: tree shake → butterfly → cast and catch → fossil → neighbour chat with voice → store → furnishing → museum donation → summer lanterns and fireworks → the bus singing → City neon, salon, auction paddle → soup → snowman → Long Night → logo. Beats of 6–10 s, 2–3 minutes a loop, a \"DEMO\" tag and pulsing prompt (touch-only: \"Controller or keyboard required\").\n734\t\n735\t**Pause (Options/Esc):** Resume · Map · Almanac · Settings · Save & Quit to Title · About · **Dev Mode**.\n736\t\n737\t**Settings:** Music (**default 10%**), Effects, Voices, Voice style, Text speed, **Controller glyphs: PlayStation or Xbox**, Rumble, Camera shake, Clock: Village Time or Real Clock, Fullscreen (`requestFullscreen`), Picture: pixel size and quality through `engine.settings`, Reset to defaults.\n738\t\n739\t**Dev mode** (from the pause menu; never in a session). Paged, pad-playable, still big type.\n740\t\n741\t| Page | Tools |\n742\t|---|---|\n743\t| Time | Set hour, next day, set day 1–16 or season, freeze, clock mode |\n744\t| World | Weather, teleport to any place or neighbour, noclip, free camera, zoom out overview |\n745\t| Player | Beans ±, clear debt, set home tier, give any item, all tools, all emotes, set look |\n746\t| Neighbours | Friendship, force favour, move in/out now, show routines |\n747\t| Events | Start any festival, contest, concert, Inspector, cheese day, wasps, shooting star, ending |\n748\t| Collections | Fill or empty museum, Almanac |\n749\t| Debug | Overlays for collision tiles, nav paths, target, NPC state; engine state line; look switcher |\n750\t| Save | Save, load, reset, export/import JSON, export recording, replay it |\n751\t| **Balance** | Live sliders with **reset per slider and Reset All**, defaults below |\n752\t\n753\t| Slider | Default | Range |\n754\t|---|---|---|\n755\t| Game minutes per real second | 1.25 | 0.25–20 |\n756\t| Walk / jog / run speed (m/s) | 2.6 / 4.2 / 6.0 | 1–6 / 2–8 / 3–12 |\n757\t| Camera zoom | 1.6 | 1.2–2.4 |\n758\t| Fish per water acre | 3 | 0–10 |\n759\t| Bite window (s) | 0.6 | 0.2–2 |\n760\t| Max false nibbles | 3 | 0–6 |\n761\t| Bugs per acre | 1.5 | 0–6 |\n762\t| Bug spook radius at a run (m) | 3 | 0.5–8 |\n763\t| Fruit regrow (days) | 2 | 0–6 |\n764\t| Weeds per day | 3 | 0–20 |\n765\t| Hybrid chance | 0.15 | 0–1 |\n766\t| Balloons per day | 3 | 0–20 |\n767\t| Sell price × / buy price × | 1 / 1 | 0.2–5 |\n768\t| Debt × | 1 | 0.2–5 |\n769\t| Favour reward × | 1 | 0–5 |\n770\t| Friendship gain × | 1 | 0–5 |\n771\t| Neighbour walk speed (m/s) | 1.8 | 0.5–5 |\n772\t| Neighbour chat rate × | 1 | 0–5 |\n773\t| Rain chance × | 1 | 0–3 |\n774\t| Cheese volatility × | 1 | 0–3 |\n775\t| Text speed (chars/s) | 35 | 10–200 |\n776\t\n777\tAll in one `src/dev/tuning.js` (`{ id, label, default, min, max, step, page }`), read live by the systems; values persist apart from the save.\n778\t\n779\t**About/Credits.** Paged, big type, reachable from title and pause:\n780\t\n781\t- **Source Information** with every field TASK.md lists, in its order (Usage reads \"added on import\").\n782\t- **Testing Feedback**, **Engine Feedback** and **Prompt Feedback**, each question and answer.\n783\t- Cast and credits, plus licences: the kit's `CREDITS.md` ships in the page (a `text/plain` script) and is readable on a Licences page; Kenney packs are CC0, credit them anyway.\n784\t- \"Inspired by Animal Crossing: City Folk\" appears once here.\n785\t\n786\tThe same text goes in your final chat reply and the exit interview.\n787\t\n788\t**Saves.** `localStorage`, JSON with a `v` field and migrations, autosaved on every door, each game hour, and at the bed. In a session it's the client's memory storage: don't special-case it, just don't load or show saves there. **Inspector Pimm**, a hermit crab who lives in a filing cabinet, appears if the last session ended without bed or Save & Quit: three escalating monologues over a player's lifetime, then one last resigned one. Autosave means this is only comedy; nothing is ever lost.\n789\t\n790\t---\n791\t\n792\t## 21. Code architecture\n793\t\n794\t```\n795\tsrc/main.js           AIHub.active? round : normal start; touch and WebGPU checks\n796\tsrc/game.js           createGame(): the one Game object and its hooks; modes: title, attract, play, round\n797\tsrc/core/             clock, calendar, weather, rng, events, storage, save (pure, no document)\n798\tsrc/world/            tiles, townMap, cityMap, collide, nav, terrainMesh, seasons, occluders, interiors\n799\tsrc/actors/           rig, clips, player, npc, schedule, crowd"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7fc0c99-6050-4a36-bbad-13de512b63e9","uuid":"adbb2b22-64e4-4e60-904c-35b108d1a031","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:18:30.279Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n---\n\n## 10. Town services\n\n| Place | Who | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Store** | Mrs. Gander (goose, spectacles, waistcoat, ledger) | Section 7 |\n| **Town Hall & Post** | Clementine (capybara, unflappable, a bee lives on her head and she consults it) | Bean Jar, Lost & Found, mail, catalogue, town rating, **Village Chime** editor |\n| **Plaza** | Mayor Trundle (snail with sash) | He has been crossing the plaza to cut a ribbon since before you came, and visibly advances each day. He arrives on Festival Day of spring. |\n| **Museum** | Dr. Quill (near-sighted pangolin who rolls into a ball when overjoyed) | Section below |\n| **Mothwick's** | Madame Mothwick (dramatic moth, nibbles hems when nervous) | Clothes, hats, **pattern studio** |\n\n**Museum.** A hall and four wings you walk through, each donation appearing at once in place:\n\n- **Aquarium** with real tanks and swimming fish, **Insectarium** with live flutter.\n- **Fossil Hall:** two original composite skeletons (Moatosaur: skull, torso, tail; Long-Necked Regretodon: skull, neck, torso) plus singles (trilobite, ammonite, amber fly, mystery tooth): 10 pieces.\n- **Gallery:** 8 parody paintings of public-domain works: *Mona Llama, Goose with a Pearl Earring, The Great Wave Off Beans, The Starry Bus Stop, The Birth of Venus Flytrap, The Honk, Whistler's Moose, The Cheese of Earthly Delights.*\n\nDr. Quill misidentifies everything first (\"A fossil! Or a very confident potato!\") and appraises unidentified fossils. Filling a wing earns a plaque with your name, misspelled.\n\n**Village Chime.** Sixteen steps on a chunky note pad, played on the Old Bell every hour, at doors, and at the finale. A player's first act of authorship; the Hall asks for it on Day 3. Also used as the store door jingle.\n\n**Clothes and patterns.**\n- Clothes floor 20 → target 30 (palette plus simple textures), hats 8 → 12, glasses 3 → 6, umbrellas 3 → 6.\n- **Pattern studio:** a 16×16 pixel editor with the 16 palette colours, 8 save slots, pen, fill, mirror, undo, playable by pad or mouse with a big grid and live preview on your avatar.\n- Patterns go on shirts, umbrellas, signs and ground tiles, and on your **festival firework**. Stored as texture data, shown with `DataTexture` + `pixelTexture` + `toonMaterial(white, { map })`.\n\n**Town rating.** Clementine gives one honest sentence and one hint (trees 40–70, flowers, weeds, litter). A high rating on the last night turns the Old Bell gold.\n\n---\n\n## 11. The City\n\n**The bus.** Press ✕ at the stop; it arrives in seconds, and the ride is a 20-second skippable scene (the bus rocking through a seasonal countryside diorama). **Hester**, a llama driver, sings a ballad about your recent life in gibberish with subtitles, made from memory events: *\"♪ Oh, you fished up an old boot and you sold it for ten, / and you'd do it, you'd do it, you'd do it again… ♪\"* Floor 20 verse templates, plus choruses. The fare is listening.\n\n**The place.** An L-shaped boulevard around a plaza, about 40×24 tiles, in its own Group. **Day:** bright, busy. **Night:** the signature look: dark street, bright glass, neon. Use `glow` parts, real colored pooled lights at each front, and a whole-scene `bloom` filter. **Bloom's default strength blows out characters and glass; `{ id: 'bloom', params: { amount: 0.3 } }` in the look's `scene` stack keeps them readable** [verified by render]. City nights only, swapped under a transition. Signs are 3D: letters built from your bitmap font as small `glow` boxes (about 0.08 m per pixel), so they read as neon.\n\n**Crowd.** 12–18 pedestrians of assorted species built from the critter generator, walking the boulevard with short overheard lines; a street musician; pigeons (`Boids`) that scatter when you run. Crowd murmur is low-volume gibberish.\n\n**Venues** (each one room, each with its own music and at least one interaction that isn't a menu; open from your first visit, Magda's only after dark):\n\n| Venue | Host | What makes it fun |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Snip & Gossip** (salon) | Monsieur Fleece, an alpaca with magnificent hair | Live preview of hair and colour on your avatar on a turntable; he tells you one piece of gossip per cut |\n| **Maison Plume** (couture) | Flavia Rosé, a flamingo behind a velvet rope | **Fashion check:** a daily theme (\"Something damp\"); your outfit's tags are judged aloud, 0–5 plumes; plumes unlock the couture rail |\n| **Magda's Genuine Articles** (after dark, down an alley) | Magda, a magpie | Three paintings a visit at a premium, some forged. Compare with the catalogue print (a missing star, an extra earring). Forgeries get a very disappointed Dr. Quill. Rare furniture too. \"One hundred percent genuine, and only forty percent stolen.\" |\n| **The Palace Theatre** (evenings) | Sir Reginald Bellows, a hippo thespian | A short sketch per visit; press Laugh on the punchline to learn an **emote** (8 in all: laugh, cry, shock, anger, love, shrug, bow, dance) |\n| **Pemberton's Auction Rooms** (10:00–18:00) | Pemberton, a dormouse who speaks at double speed | Three lots. Raise your paddle against three AI bidders with hidden limits and a \"going once…\" clock. You can also consign an item; the result arrives by mail |\n| **Better Burrows Bureau** | Agent Brisket, a badger | Explains home scoring, critiques your home in person, gives grade rewards |\n| **The Daily Grind** (café) | Languid Lou, a sloth who barely speaks | Three blends: a coffee gives a jog-speed buff until noon, and Lou's three words of the day hint at a rare catch |\n| **Madame Nibbles' tent** (plaza) | a goat who eats her tarot cards mid-reading | A daily fortune with half of it chewed off: *\"I see a tall stranger. He's holding… (chew) …that part's gone now. Good luck, though.\"* It nudges luck (fish, finds, friendship) until morning |\n\n---\n\n## 12. The year\n\n| Day | Season, type | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Spring, Market | Arrival, errands, tools (section 13) |\n| 2 | Spring, Errand | Museum opens with your fossil; Axe and Slingshot go on sale at the store; first City trip |\n| 3 | Spring, Show | Compose the Chime; Vesper plays at 20:00 |\n| 4 | Spring, **Festival** | **The Seed Toss**; the Mayor arrives |\n| 5 | Summer, Market | Season turn; Old Rind's cheese |\n| 6 | Summer, Errand | **Fishing Derby** |\n| 7 | Summer, Show | Rocco talks about leaving; Vesper |\n| 8 | Summer, **Festival** | **The Lantern Float** |\n| 9 | Autumn, Market | Rocco leaves; Fitch moves in |\n| 10 | Autumn, Errand | **Bug-Off**; mushrooms in the woods |\n| 11 | Autumn, Show | Vesper; soup ingredients start to matter |\n| 12 | Autumn, **Festival** | **The Great Soup** |\n| 13 | Winter, Market | First snow |\n| 14 | Winter, Errand | **Snowman Contest** |\n| 15 | Winter, Show | A letter from Rocco; Vesper |\n| 16 | Winter, **Festival** | **The Long Night**, year-end, credits |\n\n**Show nights.** **Vesper**, a fruit bat, plays tiny accordion hanging upside down from a plaza lamp post (\"That's how the notes fall out.\"). Ask for a mood; she plays one of 6 songs, sings in gibberish, and gives you the record for your stereo. Neighbours gather and sway.\n\n**Festivals** (each a full-village set piece with decorations and its own music):\n\n- **The Seed Toss (spring).** Everyone flings seeds at chalked rings for 90 seconds. Flowers burst from every hit; the town blooms the next morning in the pattern you sowed. The Mayor finally reaches the ribbon, and it's gone.\n- **The Lantern Float (summer).** At dusk, pick a lantern colour and a one-line wish, set it on the river at the bridge, and watch hundreds of lights drift to the sea. Fireworks over the beach at 21:00, **including one in your own pattern**. Your wish gets a reply by letter.\n- **The Great Soup (autumn).** Dorrit's giant pot on the plaza. Add three ingredients: fish, fruit, mushroom, boot. It's rated from their tags, with neighbours tasting aloud. Good soup means a feast and a reward; bad soup means a village of queasy faces and a gag-gift next day.\n- **The Long Night (winter).** The sun sets at 16:00. Carry a string of lights to each door, exchange a gift, then gather at the Old Pear at midnight as it lights up and the Bell plays your Chime, harmonised. Rocco rolls in. Clementine reads the town rating, and at a high rating the Bell turns gold. An almanac of your year (biggest catch, most-sold-to, debt status, who you talked to most) leads into credits. Year Two begins next morning with a letter.\n\n**Contests** (timed 3 game hours; neighbours post real scores from the same rules; trophy furniture for placing): Fishing Derby, biggest fish; Bug-Off, most value; Snowman Contest, best proportions, judged by Bartleby, who would prefer not.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Day One\n\nThe first 20 minutes teach by errand. Clock at half speed until done.\n\n1. **Title → New Arrival.** Choose Village Time or Real Clock in one line each.\n2. **The bus.** Night, rain, Hester. \"End of the line? Nobody gets off there on purpose.\" She asks your name, what the town's called (\"I only drive the bus\"), then: *What are you running from? Favourite weather? How do you take bad news?* Answers set hair, colours and eyes. A reflection in the bus window lets you adjust the look. \"You look like someone who owes money.\"\n3. **The stop.** Mrs. Gander, ledger open: \"You're late. No, you're exactly on time. I've already billed you for being late.\" She walks you to a roofless shack. \"The roof is included. The rain is complimentary. The 8,000 beans are not.\" Apron on: you work it off.\n4. **Errand list** (a short HUD list; each takes about 2 minutes and pays off part of the debt):\n   - Fetch a parcel from Dorrit (talk, map, soup joke).\n   - Pull weeds, sell one at the store (pick up, sell).\n   - Tavi gives you a **Net**: catch a butterfly (sneak, swing, reveal).\n   - Bartleby has a **Rod** and would prefer not: catch him a fish anyway (cast, bite).\n   - Mayor Trundle lends a **Shovel**, already on his way somewhere: dig the crack on the plaza (dig, fossil).\n   - **Watering can** from Clementine: water the Mayor's sapling.\n   - Write to Gran at Post (letter UI, on-screen keyboard).\n5. **Evening.** \"You've paid off 2,000 beans. You're free to go. Not that you were ever not free. Legally.\" Dusk, lit windows, first hourly chime. Bed saves. Gran's reply and a neighbour's letter are in the mailbox next morning.\n\nEvery errand demonstrates a verb. Wandering off is fine: the list waits.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Writing bible\n\n**Voice.** Warm, deadpan, gently absurd. Everyone is a bit rude and it's affectionate. Short lines. Jokes land at the end of the sentence. Sincerity is allowed, rarely, and hits harder for it. Capitalism is a fond joke. No irony about the player's feelings. British-or-American spelling: pick one and hold it.\n\n**Samples to match:**\n\n- **Mrs. Gander:** \"No rush on the payment, dear. I've simply noted the time you didn't pay, and I'll be noting it again tomorrow.\" / \"HONK. Pardon me. That was my indoor voice.\"\n- **Mayor Trundle:** \"I declare this… ribbon… cut. Has anyone… seen… the ribbon? It was here… in March.\"\n- **Clementine:** \"Your letter's stamped. A bee has been living in the stamp drawer. We've agreed to share.\"\n- **Dr. Quill:** \"Is it a fossil? It's a fossil! Or a very confident potato! Either way, it goes in the hall!\"\n- **Dorrit:** \"You look peaky, mmhm. I made soup. It's Tuesday's. Tuesday was a good day for soup, mmhm.\"\n- **Tavi:** \"I smiled at a rock today and I think it's going really well, isn't it?!\"\n- **Bartleby:** \"You're standing in my good spot. No, there isn't another one. They're all my good spot. I'd prefer not.\"\n- **Lady Ottoline:** \"I am technically the Duchess of this river, darling dear. Yes, that one. No, don't touch it.\"\n- **Rocco:** \"I rolled here! From over there! That's thirty metres of pure cardio, let's roll!\"\n- **Nib:** \"I wrote you a poem. It's about your hat. It ends badly, or so the moon says.\"\n- **Ozzie:** \"Have you ever *seen* Mrs. Gander eat? No. Neither has anyone. You didn't hear it from me.\"\n- **Yolanda:** \"Hon, I knitted you a thing. Not sure what it is yet. Give it time, hon.\"\n- **Inspector Pimm:** \"Pimm. Department of Continuity. You left. No bed. No goodbye. Do you know what that does to a Tuesday?\"\n- **Vesper:** \"Sit anywhere, friend. I'd offer you a chair, but I'm using the ceiling.\"\n- **Flavia Rosé:** \"Darling. Stand in better lighting. No. Further. Keep going.\"\n- **Old Rind:** \"Psst. Wheel of cheese? It only goes up. Mostly. Until it goes off.\"\n\n**Volumes** (floor → target, total about 700 → 1,200 lines):\n\n| Bank | Lines |\n|---|---|\n| Per type: greetings, weather, time of day, season, absent, hungry, gift reactions, favours, letters | 40 → 80 each (8 types) |\n| Unique per neighbour | 12 → 30 each |\n| Gossip templates | 30 → 60 |\n| Mrs. Gander | 30 |\n| Each other named host | 15–30 |\n\n**Mechanics.** Lines are data: `{ id, text, when: { season, weather, hour, friendMin, memory }, weight }`, with tags `{player} {town} {item} {neighbour} {catch}`, `|` for a beat, `*word*` for emphasis, and name colours. Choices are 2–4 options. Each line should be seen once before it repeats. Neighbours speak with their tic often but not always.\n\n---\n\n## 15. UI and UX\n\nController first, on a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse get the same screens and should feel native.\n\n| Action | DualSense (Xbox glyph) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move | Left stick: tilt sets tiptoe, walk, jog | WASD or arrows | Left-click ground to walk there; hold to follow the cursor |\n| Run | Hold ○ (B) | Hold Shift | |\n| Sneak | Light tilt | Hold Ctrl | |\n| Talk, open, pick up, shake, confirm | ✕ (A) | E or Enter | Left-click the thing (walks into reach first) |\n| Use tool | □ (X) | Space or F | Right-click where you're aiming |\n| Back | ○ (B) | Esc or Backspace | Right-click in menus |\n| Pockets | △ (Y) | Tab or I | Click its icon |\n| Previous, next tool | L1, R1 (LB, RB) | 1–6 | Wheel |\n| Turn camera ±90° | L2, R2 (LT, RT) or right-stick flick | Q, R | |\n| Zoom step | Right-stick up, down | + − | |\n| Map | Touchpad press or D-pad up | M | Click |\n| Almanac | D-pad down | J | Click |\n| Emotes | D-pad left | G | Click |\n| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc | |\n\nCreate stays unused: **Create + Options belongs to the Hub.** Rumble via `vibrationActuator` (bite, dig, thunder) where available.\n\n**Prompts** follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs for controllers (✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1 L2 R2 Options, drawn as pixel sprites), Xbox glyphs (A B X Y LB RB LT RT Menu) with a Settings option, keycaps and mouse-button icons for keyboard and mouse. Corner prompt shows at most three. **Mouse:** a pixel cursor (OS cursor hidden); hover moves focus in menus and highlights things in the world with a name tooltip; click activates.\n\n**HUD.** Date and time top left, beans top right, tool bottom left, context prompt bottom right. It fades after a few idle seconds and returns on change. Safe margins: 12 art pixels on all sides.\n\n**Dialogue.** Bottom panel, three lines, nameplate, typewriter with voice, skip to end then advance on ✕. The camera eases in a little (`setCamera` tween to zoom 2.0). The speaker faces you.\n\n**Screens** (each is a focused single idea; **never a table**, never tiny text, no scrollbars, pages instead, at most 8 focusable things except the grids below):\n\n| Screen | Form |\n|---|---|\n| Pockets | 5×3 big cells (art ×2), selected item's name and one sentence, actions on press |\n| Tool ring | Pops around you while cycling |\n| Map | Pixel map, pins, who's where now |\n| Almanac | Tabs: Fish, Bugs, Fossils, Art, Stars, Calendar; big silhouettes that fill in |\n| Letter | A sheet of paper with big type; on-screen keyboard grid (pad), typing (keyboard) |\n| Name entry | Same keyboard, plus △ for a random suggestion |\n| Catalogue | One big item card at a time, by category |\n| Placement | In-room grid, nothing else on screen |\n| Pattern studio | Big 16×16 grid, palette strip, preview |\n| Chime | 16 tall chunky columns |\n| Emote wheel | Eight wedges |\n\n**Icons.** About 45 category sprites (16×16 rows, recoloured per item: 4 fish shapes, 5 bug shapes, chair, lamp, shirt…). Don't try to author one per item.\n\n**Touch-only devices** (`touchOnly` above, no pad connected): **no touch controls.** The title shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button (⚙). The attract mode still runs after 10 seconds; a tap exits it. A key press or pad press clears the notice and the game plays normally.\n\n**The engine's ⚙ panel's Reset sets music back to 60%** (its default). On touch-only devices, where the panel is reachable, wrap `audio.resetVolumes` on the instance so it restores 10%. Elsewhere, the game's own Settings replaces the panel.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Art direction\n\n**Base style:** the engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art with the default look (`LOOK_PRESETS.none`): toon bands, pixel outlines, Sweetie 16. Rendered comparisons: `none` and `pixel_world` read best, `dream` blooms too much, `cel_cartoon` stops feeling like pixel art. Use `none`.\n\n**Readability rules.** Quiet ground, loud actors. Dark outlines. Chunky shapes (≥ 0.25 m, per the guide). Doors, windows and signs are the most saturated things on a building. Reference proportions [verified]: cottage walls 4.4×2.2×3.6 m with two wedge roofs and a chimney; critters 1.5 m with a 0.9 m head; `lamp-post`, `notice-board`, `park-bench`, `market-stall`, `fountain` from the library work as they are.\n\n**New assets to make (they're what makes it feel like this game):**\n\n- **Critter generator** (`src/art/critter.js`): parts for head shape, ears, antlers, bills, tails, shell, hair, clothes, from one function. All neighbours, hosts and crowd come from it. Each needs a distinct silhouette at 45 px: moose antlers with laundry, platypus bill and tiara, armadillo bands, lemur ringed tail (`SpringChain`), yak hair curtain, snail shell and sash, goose neck and spectacles, bat hanging, hermit crab in a filing cabinet, flamingo legs, alpaca hair.\n- **Buildings:** home ×4 tiers, store ×3, Town Hall with Post flag, Museum with columns, Mothwick's with giant spool, 9 cottages (shared shell, personal decoration), bus and shelter, 8 City fronts with neon, City skyline, lighthouse.\n- **Nature:** own trees (pear, pine, palm, plus stages) with seasonal palettes and fruit, flowers (4 species), weeds, saplings, beanstalk, Old Pear (huge), Old Bell, river banks, cliffs, ramps, bridges.\n- **Items:** tools; fish and bugs (parametric: body, fins, colour; library `butterfly`/`firefly` sprites are fine for flyers); fossils; 8 paintings (pixel `TextureDef`s with a forgery variant); hats; furniture signature pieces; cheese wheel; snowman; lanterns; balloon.\n\n**Life everywhere.** Blinking, tails, smoke, swaying crowns, cloud shadows, birds (`Boids`), fish rings, laundry, butterflies, puddles, petals, leaves, lit windows. If a screen is ever still for 3 seconds, something's missing. Performance is out of scope: add what the scene needs.\n\n**Every sheet goes through** `node check.mjs --sheets` from your kit (exit 0, no problems; warnings fixed unless you mean them). **Look at the PNGs in the game too.**\n\n---\n\n## 17. Animation\n\n**System:** the engine's clip format (`ClipDef` data at 30 fps, full poses, easing, sine layers), compiled with `compileClips` onto the 7-joint rig, played with `THREE.AnimationMixer`, cross-faded with `crossFadeTo`. Plus `Squash` (stop, land, pick up), `SpringChain` (tails, ears, ribbons), code-driven face parts, and head turns applied after the mixer.\n\n**Clips (floor 30):** Idle, IdleBored, Walk, Jog, Run, Tiptoe, Talk, Listen, Nod, ShakeHead, Laugh, Cry, Shock, Anger, Love, Shrug, Bow, Dance, Wave, Sit, Sleep, PlayDead, HoldUp, PickUp, Give, NetReady, NetSweep, Cast, Reel, Dig, Chop, Water, ShakeTree, Slingshot, PushSnow, Eat, Hum, Sway, Sting, Slip, plus species specials: Mayor's slide, Vesper hanging, Pimm's sideways scuttle, Rocco's roll.\n\n**Feel notes.** Net: 3 frames of anticipation then a fast arc. Tree shake: canopy wobble and leaves. Dig: three dirt chunks, then a hole. Bite: bobber snaps under. HoldUp: both arms, item above head, pause. Dialogue: small nods and talk mouth on syllables. `validateClip(clip, RIG)` returns nothing in tests; check `renderSheet` contact sheets for a few key clips.\n\n---\n\n## 18. Audio\n\nEverything is code: no audio files. Music plays through the engine's `Song` sequencer, effects through `SoundDef`s.\n\n**Music volume defaults to 10%.** The engine's default is 60%, stored under `pixel-engine:audio`. On a profile with no stored audio, call `audio.setVolume('music', 0.1)` before any music starts. **Author tracks hot**: make simultaneous track volumes sum to about 1.0 [verified numbers, section 2], or 10% is inaudible. Settings Reset restores 10%. Test it.\n\n**Music** (all new; easygoing, a little jazzy, lots of walking bass; `stepsPerBeat: 3` gives a swing feel):\n\n| Set | Count (floor → target) |\n|---|---|\n| **Hourly tunes:** every one of the 24 hours has its own arrangement, each with rain and snow variants, built by `composer.js` from hand-written motifs (transposition, tempo, re-voicing) | 8 → 12 motifs |\n| Title, credits, bus ride, bed lullaby | 4 |\n| Venues: store, museum, Mothwick's, Hall, City day, City night, salon, couture, Magda's, theatre, auction, café, Bureau | 13 |\n| Festivals, contests, season stings | 4 + 1 + 4 |\n| Vesper's records | 6 |\n\nOn the hour: Bell plays the Chime, then the new tune starts. Indoors, the venue's tune replaces it. At home, your stereo. `parseSong` on every song in tests.\n\n**Ambience** with `audio.loop` voices and `spatialize`: river, surf, rain, wind, crickets, birds, City hum, neon buzz.\n\n**Effects** (floor 40): footsteps per surface, each tool, splashes, bite, pick-ups, reveal jingle, UI tick, confirm, cancel, doors, mail flag, bus engine, HONK, paddle, cheese smell, firework.\n\n**Voices: gibberish, Animalese/Simlish style, code only** [method verified]. Each letter of the typed line triggers a very short syllable: a `SoundDef` of stacked sine harmonics whose weights follow vowel formants (a harmonic stack through `layers`), with noise layers for consonants.\n\n```js\nfunction vowel(f0, f1, f2, f3, dur = 0.09) {            // renderSound: peak about 0.8, 5 ms to render\n  const amp = h => { const f = f0*h, g = (c,w,a) => a*Math.exp(-((f-c)**2)/(2*w*w)); return g(f1,90,1)+g(f2,120,.6)+g(f3,180,.25); };\n  const layers = []; for (let h = 2; h*f0 < 4000; h++) { const v = amp(h)*.45; if (v > .01) layers.push({ wave:'sine', freq:f0*h, attack:.008, sustain:dur*.55, decay:dur*.45, volume:v }); }\n  return { wave:'sine', freq:f0, attack:.008, sustain:dur*.55, decay:dur*.45, volume:Math.max(.08, amp(1)*.45), vibrato:{ depth:.2, rate:18 }, layers };\n}\n```\n\nRegister about 10 syllables per voice archetype (6 archetypes); each character has a base pitch (`play(name, { pitch })`), speed and lilt; question marks rise; capitals are loud; Mrs. Gander's HONK is a square sweep; Pemberton plays at double speed; Lou is low and slow. Singing (Hester, Vesper) uses the same syllables at melody pitches. A separate **Voices** slider scales `volume` per play (the engine has only master, sfx, music channels). Settings offers Gibberish, Soft beeps or Off. Audio unlocks on first input, so attract is silent until then; `audio.counts` still counts, which tests use.\n\n---\n\n## 19. Multiplayer\n\nFollow `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md` (AI Hub Session Protocol v1); `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` sit beside it. Two modes share one host/guest code path behind a `Transport` interface: `send(data, { to, reliable })`, `on('message')`, `players`.\n\n### In an AI Hub session: shared world, \"The Bean Bazaar\"\n\nWhen `AIHub.active`, the game opens straight into the round: no title, attract, menus, pause, dev mode, saves or quit. Build only the arena. **Call `AIHub.connect` first thing, before `Engine.start`** (hello times out at 10 s). Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the arena is built (the harness allows 20 s with four software-rendered copies, so aim under 8 s) with no key press. If `connect()` answers `null`, there's no hub after all: carry on with the normal start.\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"5\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Bank the most beans before the last bell\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move: stick or WASD. Do the thing: Cross or E. Sell at the stall.\">\n<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">\n```\n\n**The round.** Five minutes on a 32×24 Fair Green (pond and river strip, small orchard, flower patch, dig cracks, a central stall), in the village's art.\n\n- All verbs are contextual on one button, no tool switching: shake, dig, cast, reel, swing, sell.\n- Five pocket slots. Unbanked value counts half at the bell, so trips to the stall matter.\n- Neighbours post short orders (\"Dorrit wants a pear: double beans\").\n- A bell announces golden moments (golden fish, a rich tree) at set times. The Mayor inches across the green; bump him for a tip of the sash and small bonus.\n- Avatars wear seat colours (`#e8443a #3b82f6 #22c55e #f5b700`) with hub names over their heads (never inserted as HTML). A scoreboard strip shows everyone.\n\n**Own card** (`connect({ mode:'shared-world', seats:[1,4], card:true })`): the plaza, players lined up in their colours, the game's name, who's ready, how to win, this player's controls, time left from the `card` event. Answers: Ready = Enter, Cross or click, `AIHub.answer('ready')`; More time = Triangle, a named key or click, `AIHub.answer('more')`. Nothing else on the card; it changes nothing. Read Enter with a plain `keydown` listener on `window`: the harness pokes a synthetic key and allows 3 s, and software-rendered frames can be slow.\n\n**Protocol duties.**\n- Play starts at `AIHub.time() >= 0`; show the 3-second countdown from `start`.\n- Host is authoritative. Bell at min(5:00, `limitMs` − 5 s): `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })` listing every player still in `AIHub.players`, ties grouped. Comic awards (\"Most Fish Apologised To\").\n- On `time-up`, finish at once.\n- On `leave`, remove the avatar.\n- On `end`, stop input, `audio.stopMusic()` and `stopLoops()`, send nothing.\n- `server` copy: `AIHub.server` true, no local player, input or avatar; slow overview camera; runs the world as usual.\n- Use `AIHub.rng(label)` for host-independent randomness.\n\n**Session rules to hold.** Network only through the hub. **No RTC or BroadcastChannel objects are even created in a session** (the check watches for sockets): load those modules only when `!AIHub.active`. No chat, emotes, typed names or `alert`/`confirm`/`prompt`/`window.open`. Ignore Options while Create is held. A touch-only player without a pad stays connected as a spectator with the notice on screen.\n\n### On its own: Visit\n\nFrom the title: **Open my gate** or **Visit a friend**. A guest arrives by bus in the host's town. Same avatars, tools and neighbours; the host's world is authoritative.\n\n| Transport | Use | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **BroadcastChannel** | Two windows on one computer, no typing | Hosts announce on a channel; guests see them listed. Same browser profile only [verified]. |\n| **WebRTC with invite codes** | Two computers, no server | A **Gate Pass** (offer) goes to the friend, who pastes it and returns a **Reply Slip** (answer). `CompressionStream('deflate-raw')` + base64 gets about 600 characters; wait up to 4 s for ICE gathering [verified]. Use a public STUN server for the internet; it still works on a LAN if offline. Paste through a pixel-styled DOM textarea (needs a keyboard, and says so); Copy with a selected-text fallback. A knowing joke about friend codes is welcome. |\n\nUp to 3 guests in a star. On join the host sends a chunked reliable town snapshot (tiles, objects, weather, time, neighbours) so the guest builds a temporary town; guests' own save holds only their pockets and beans, written on leaving. Guests may catch, pick fruit, chat through emotes and canned phrases, drop gifts; they can't fell trees, pull flowers or use the bus (\"Bus doesn't run while you have company.\"). Host leaving sends guests home.\n\n### Netcode (both modes)\n\n| Message | Direction | Rate |\n|---|---|---|\n| `me`: x, z, facing, action | guest → host, unreliable | 10 Hz |\n| `do`: action request (catch, pick, sell…) | guest → host, reliable | on action |\n| `snap`: all avatars, moving objects (≤ 1,100 bytes) | host → all, unreliable | 10 Hz |\n| `ev`: results, spawns, score, bell | host → all, reliable | on change |\n| `town`: chunks ≤ 12 KB | host → guest, reliable | once, Visit only |\n\nKeep the Hub's budget everywhere (≤ 20 messages a second, burst 40, ≤ 16 KB): `Transport` enforces it in Visit too, so one code path passes the harness. Own avatar is client-predicted; remote avatars render 120 ms in the past and snap if more than 3 m off. Catches are claimed by the client and validated by the host (still there, in reach). Tested at `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`.\n\n---\n\n## 20. Title, attract, pause, settings, dev mode, About, saves\n\n**Title.** The live village diorama with the logo (bitmap font at ×3–4). Menu: **Continue · New Arrival · Visit · Settings · About**. Prompts per device.\n\n**Attract mode.** After **10 seconds with no input on the title/menu screen** (not inside Settings, About or name entry), a looping AI-played demo starts; **any input** (key, button, stick, mouse move, tap) exits to the title at once. It runs on a throwaway demo save and never touches the player's. It cheats freely (teleports, time and season jumps behind quick irises, no clip, infinite beans). Its only job is to show as much as possible: tree shake → butterfly → cast and catch → fossil → neighbour chat with voice → store → furnishing → museum donation → summer lanterns and fireworks → the bus singing → City neon, salon, auction paddle → soup → snowman → Long Night → logo. Beats of 6–10 s, 2–3 minutes a loop, a \"DEMO\" tag and pulsing prompt (touch-only: \"Controller or keyboard required\").\n\n**Pause (Options/Esc):** Resume · Map · Almanac · Settings · Save & Quit to Title · About · **Dev Mode**.\n\n**Settings:** Music (**default 10%**), Effects, Voices, Voice style, Text speed, **Controller glyphs: PlayStation or Xbox**, Rumble, Camera shake, Clock: Village Time or Real Clock, Fullscreen (`requestFullscreen`), Picture: pixel size and quality through `engine.settings`, Reset to defaults.\n\n**Dev mode** (from the pause menu; never in a session). Paged, pad-playable, still big type.\n\n| Page | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Time | Set hour, next day, set day 1–16 or season, freeze, clock mode |\n| World | Weather, teleport to any place or neighbour, noclip, free camera, zoom out overview |\n| Player | Beans ±, clear debt, set home tier, give any item, all tools, all emotes, set look |\n| Neighbours | Friendship, force favour, move in/out now, show routines |\n| Events | Start any festival, contest, concert, Inspector, cheese day, wasps, shooting star, ending |\n| Collections | Fill or empty museum, Almanac |\n| Debug | Overlays for collision tiles, nav paths, target, NPC state; engine state line; look switcher |\n| Save | Save, load, reset, export/import JSON, export recording, replay it |\n| **Balance** | Live sliders with **reset per slider and Reset All**, defaults below |\n\n| Slider | Default | Range |\n|---|---|---|\n| Game minutes per real second | 1.25 | 0.25–20 |\n| Walk / jog / run speed (m/s) | 2.6 / 4.2 / 6.0 | 1–6 / 2–8 / 3–12 |\n| Camera zoom | 1.6 | 1.2–2.4 |\n| Fish per water acre | 3 | 0–10 |\n| Bite window (s) | 0.6 | 0.2–2 |\n| Max false nibbles | 3 | 0–6 |\n| Bugs per acre | 1.5 | 0–6 |\n| Bug spook radius at a run (m) | 3 | 0.5–8 |\n| Fruit regrow (days) | 2 | 0–6 |\n| Weeds per day | 3 | 0–20 |\n| Hybrid chance | 0.15 | 0–1 |\n| Balloons per day | 3 | 0–20 |\n| Sell price × / buy price × | 1 / 1 | 0.2–5 |\n| Debt × | 1 | 0.2–5 |\n| Favour reward × | 1 | 0–5 |\n| Friendship gain × | 1 | 0–5 |\n| Neighbour walk speed (m/s) | 1.8 | 0.5–5 |\n| Neighbour chat rate × | 1 | 0–5 |\n| Rain chance × | 1 | 0–3 |\n| Cheese volatility × | 1 | 0–3 |\n| Text speed (chars/s) | 35 | 10–200 |\n\nAll in one `src/dev/tuning.js` (`{ id, label, default, min, max, step, page }`), read live by the systems; values persist apart from the save.\n\n**About/Credits.** Paged, big type, reachable from title and pause:\n\n- **Source Information** with every field TASK.md lists, in its order (Usage reads \"added on import\").\n- **Testing Feedback**, **Engine Feedback** and **Prompt Feedback**, each question and answer.\n- Cast and credits, plus licences: the kit's `CREDITS.md` ships in the page (a `text/plain` script) and is readable on a Licences page; Kenney packs are CC0, credit them anyway.\n- \"Inspired by Animal Crossing: City Folk\" appears once here.\n\nThe same text goes in your final chat reply and the exit interview.\n\n**Saves.** `localStorage`, JSON with a `v` field and migrations, autosaved on every door, each game hour, and at the bed. In a session it's the client's memory storage: don't special-case it, just don't load or show saves there. **Inspector Pimm**, a hermit crab who lives in a filing cabinet, appears if the last session ended without bed or Save & Quit: three escalating monologues over a player's lifetime, then one last resigned one. Autosave means this is only comedy; nothing is ever lost.\n\n---\n\n## 21. Code architecture\n\n```\nsrc/main.js           AIHub.active? round : normal start; touch and WebGPU checks\nsrc/game.js           createGame(): the one Game object and its hooks; modes: title, attract, play, round\nsrc/core/             clock, calendar, weather, rng, events, storage, save (pure, no document)\nsrc/world/            tiles, townMap, cityMap, collide, nav, terrainMesh, seasons, occluders, interiors\nsrc/actors/           rig, clips, player, npc, schedule, crowd","numLines":400,"startLine":400,"totalLines":925}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018xRE7fYKdTh7veKUQEhyez","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkiQwcYqyrvwyma8LjeE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017FnreoeENSpqcZwiSGFnBg","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/PLAN.md","offset":800},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15203,"cache_read_input_tokens":40687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15203},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7fc0c99-6050-4a36-bbad-13de512b63e9","uuid":"f8df6191-20f0-4552-a2f3-9a6246c6a992","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:18:31.830Z","request_id":"req_011CgkiQwNugaCZmgBKjnfcH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017FnreoeENSpqcZwiSGFnBg":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/PLAN.md","offset":800}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017FnreoeENSpqcZwiSGFnBg","type":"tool_result","content":"800\tsrc/systems/          tools, fishing, bugs, dig, trees, flowers, stars, snow, economy, cheese, shop,\n801\t                      museum, home, placement, rating, mail, favours, memory, town rating,\n802\t                      salon, couture, magda, theatre, auction, bureau, cafe, fortune, bus,\n803\t                      festivals/ (seedToss, lanternFloat, soup, longNight), contests\n804\tsrc/ui/               font, glyphs, panel, focus, prompts, keyboard, dialogue, screens/*\n805\tsrc/audio/            voice, composer, songs/*, sfx, ambience\n806\tsrc/data/             items, fish, bugs, fossils, art, furniture, clothes, neighbours, dialogue/*, festivals, year\n807\tsrc/art/              critter, models, textures (everything exported for check.mjs --sheets)\n808\tsrc/net/              transport, hub, broadcast, rtc, host, guest, arena, visit, protocol\n809\tsrc/dev/              tuning, devmode, attract\n810\ttests/                run.mjs, sim/*, play/*, browser/*, hub/\n811\ttools/                build.mjs\n812\t```\n813\t\n814\t- **State:** one plain JSON `world` object is the save. Systems are functions over `world` plus `ctx`; presentation reads it. An event bus (`caught`, `sold`, `gift`, `talked`, `day`) feeds memory, stats and tests.\n815\t- **Data-driven:** adding a fish, neighbour, furniture set, festival, venue or line needs no code, only a data entry and possibly a model def, so the rest of the game can be built later with normal designer tools. Map rows, dialogue, items, songs, tuning and the year table are all plain data; dev mode's Events page lists them automatically.\n816\t- **UI:** a screen stack of plain objects (`open`, `input`, `draw`); focus and hit areas are real data, hover and click resolve against them; `ui.model()` returns the current screen, focus and prompts for tests.\n817\t- **Test handle:** `window.__GAME__` (installed in the `ready` hook) exposes `world`, `ui.model()`, `tuning`, and actions; `status()` gives day, hour, place, beans, debt.\n818\t- **Replays:** `fingerprint()` hashes day, minute, position, beans, debt and collection counts.\n819\t\n820\t---\n821\t\n822\t## 22. Testing\n823\t\n824\t**One command:** `node tests/run.mjs` prints only failures and a one-line count. `--only sim|play|browser|hub|visit|sheets` for one suite. Rerun a suite only after changing what it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Keep one or two browsers open at once and close them; keep all files in your folder; stop only your own PIDs.\n825\t\n826\t| Suite | Runs | Checks |\n827\t|---|---|---|\n828\t| **sim** | Node, under 10 s | Calendar both clocks, Real Clock mapping, weather table, drop tables, cheese patterns, flower rules, debt math, save round trip and migration, map flood fill, line tags resolve, every `Song` parses, every clip validates, voice syllables render and peak under 1 |\n829\t| **play** | `Engine.headless`, under 3 minutes | **Scripted playthrough of the whole game** (below), then replay and compare `fingerprint()` |\n830\t| **browser** | Chromium on `file://` the built HTML, with the flags from section 2 (not TASK.md's shorter list) | Boots with no errors; engine text unchanged in the page; fresh profile `audio.volume.music === 0.1`, and after Settings Reset; stub pad drives title → play → pause; keyboard and mouse the same; prompts switch glyphs with last device, Xbox option works; attract starts at 10 s, a key, a button, a mouse move each exit it, player save untouched; analyser on `audio.master` shows music and voice levels above silence (use `page.keyboard.press` first to unlock); dev mode opens from pause, a slider changes behaviour, Reset All restores; About has every field; Chromium phone and tablet profiles: no touch UI, notice and ⚙ showing, tap opens settings, attract runs; WebKit: friendly no-WebGPU message and no uncaught error |\n831\t| **hub** | Harness (below) | `window.__hubCheck.pass` in every scenario, each run asserted |\n832\t| **visit** | Two pages in one context | BroadcastChannel join and movement seen on both; WebRTC with `iceServers: []` join, invite codes round trip; after leaving, the guest keeps pockets and beans and the host's save is unchanged |\n833\t| **sheets** | `node check.mjs --sheets src/art/models.js` from the kit | Exit 0 |\n834\t\n835\t**Hub harness.** `tools/build.mjs` copies the HTML to `tests/hub/game.html` beside unmodified `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`. Serve that folder with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` and read the port from its first line (`-u`, or it never prints through a pipe). Open `harness.html?game=game.html&auto=1&` plus each of:\n836\t\n837\t- `players=2`\n838\t- `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`\n839\t- `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&scenario=host-leave`\n840\t- `players=2&scenario=server`\n841\t\n842\tWait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`. Confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop the server by its PID in a `finally`.\n843\t\n844\t**Playthrough bot** (`tests/play/`): helpers `walkTo`, `talk`, `pressUI`, `useTool`, `sleepTo`, `nextDay`, driving `input.setKey`/`setPointer` on virtual codes through real input, `engine.step` in batches. Raise game speed for dull stretches. Milestones to assert:\n845\t\n846\t1. New Arrival through bus: name, town, look set; debt 8,000.\n847\t2. Day One errands done; each tool gained; a letter sent; reply next morning.\n848\t3. Fish, bug, fossil caught; a fish sold; beans and memory updated; a neighbour line references it.\n849\t4. Tree shaken, sapling planted, flower watered, hybrid formed, weed pulled, balloon shot, wasp sting and cure, bag buried and beanstalk tripled, shooting-star wish delivered.\n850\t5. Museum donation appears in each wing; Almanac updates.\n851\t6. Each debt tier paid; home tier changes; placement, wallpaper, lamp, stereo, mirror, TV, Bureau grade by mail.\n852\t7. City: every venue entered, one interaction each (cut, plume check, forgery spotted, emote learned, auction won, consignment mailed, critique, coffee buff, fortune).\n853\t8. Cheese bought, sold at profit, goes bad.\n854\t9. Chime composed, heard on the hour; Vesper's record plays on the stereo; pattern drawn, worn, and in the firework.\n855\t10. All four festivals and three contests complete with results. Rocco leaves, Fitch arrives, Rocco returns.\n856\t11. Bed options, Hibernate jump, season reskins, absence effects (faked timestamp), Inspector Pimm on unclean exit.\n857\t12. Long Night, gold Bell at high rating, credits, Year Two starts.\n858\t\n859\t**Stand-ins** are as TASK.md lists: stub `navigator.getGamepads` in `add_init_script`, Playwright touch profiles, Web Audio analysers, two pages. `e.step()` draws nothing: for a picture, set `e.manual = false` and let a frame draw. The software GPU runs 10–20 fps at 1080p, so drive logic in manual time and don't rely on wall-clock speed. Judge the 10-foot view from 1920×1080 screenshots.\n860\t\n861\t**Look at screenshots** for: title, dialogue, Pockets, each season, night, interior, City night, one festival. Check that UI text is legible and actors stand out.\n862\t\n863\t---\n864\t\n865\t## 23. Build order and cut rules\n866\t\n867\tEach step leaves something playable.\n868\t\n869\t1. **Scaffold and packaging.** Title chosen. `tools/build.mjs`, single HTML boots on `file://` with a test scene, test runner printing a count.\n870\t2. **UI kit and input.** Font, panels, focus, glyphs, last-device, virtual pad codes, screen stack, keyboard, settings shell, music 10%.\n871\t3. **Village walk.** Map data, terrain, season surfaces, collision, camera, turns, occluders, wind, one cottage, your avatar and rig.\n872\t4. **Neighbours talk.** Critter generator, clips, routines, dialogue, voices. By now it should be charming. If it isn't, fix it before anything else.\n873\t5. **Verbs.** Tools, fishing, bugs, digging, trees, flowers, reveal, puns, Pockets.\n874\t6. **Time.** Clocks, calendar, weather, seasons, day and night, bed, absence, hourly music.\n875\t7. **Economy and home.** Store, debt, Bean Jar, placement, interactive furniture, Day One script, mail.\n876\t8. **Town services.** Museum, Hall, Mothwick's, patterns, Chime, town rating, Almanac, map.\n877\t9. **The City.** Bus and ballads, boulevard, crowd, neon, all venues.\n878\t10. **The year.** Festivals, contests, Vesper, cheese, Rocco's story, ending, Inspector Pimm.\n879\t11. **Multiplayer.** Hub round and harness suite first, then Visit.\n880\t12. **Shell.** Attract, dev mode, About, final title flow, touch-only.\n881\t13. **Full playthrough suite, polish, content to target.** Then the finish checklist.\n882\t\n883\t**If effort runs short:** cut content to floors first. Next, simplify a venue's fun to a smaller interaction, not to a bare menu. **Never cut:** Day One, neighbours and memory, City, the four festivals, the 10% music rule, controller/keyboard/mouse parity, Settings, attract, dev mode with sliders, Hub pass, About, exit interview. If one Visit transport can't be finished, remove its menu entry entirely: TASK.md allows no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. Anything unfinished must not be visible.\n884\t\n885\t---\n886\t\n887\t## 24. Finish checklist and TASK.md coverage\n888\t\n889\t**Before you finish:**\n890\t\n891\t- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs` shows zero failures; output of the last full run is real, not remembered.\n892\t- [ ] Single HTML in the place TASK.md names, opens by double-click on `file://`, no network needed. No TODOs, placeholders or stubs anywhere (`grep`).\n893\t- [ ] About/Credits filled with Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, the same text as your chat reply. \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Usage says \"added on import\".\n894\t- [ ] Exit interview file written in exactly TASK.md's shape, parses as JSON. Suggested `aiHub` values: type `Game`, genre `Life Sim`, source `Animal Crossing: City Folk`, series `Animal Crossing`, players `1–4`, tags from `pixel art`, `3d`, `comedy`, `exploration`, `versus`, plus your name, tagline, description and controls line.\n895\t- [ ] Every process you started is stopped; no stray files outside your folder.\n896\t\n897\t**Where this plan answers each line of TASK.md:**\n898\t\n899\t| TASK.md | Here |\n900\t|---|---|\n901\t| Source | Header, section 1 |\n902\t| Genre, Perspective | Section 1 |\n903\t| Game Title (riff, 50 ideas, real RNG) | Sections 0, 1; left to the builder |\n904\t| Research | Done for this plan; further research is your choice |\n905\t| Package: single HTML, dependencies | Section 2 |\n906\t| Tech Stack: riftlight kit, engine as ships | Section 2 |\n907\t| Art Direction | Section 16 |\n908\t| Animation | Section 17 |\n909\t| Audio: code only, gibberish voices, music 10% | Section 18 |\n910\t| Online; Multiplayer: Hub and on its own | Section 19 |\n911\t| Working environment and deliverable, shared machine | Sections 0, 2, 22 |\n912\t| This computer, its stand-ins, sharing it | Sections 2, 22 (and one correction: browser flags, section 2) |\n913\t| Performance out of scope, unlimited budget | Sections 16, 22 |\n914\t| Testing: headless, one command, playthrough, no debate subagents | Section 22 |\n915\t| Effort | Your call; section 23 orders it |\n916\t| Goal and scope: original, feature-complete vertical slice, designer-extensible | Sections 1, 12, 21 |\n917\t| Dev mode from pause, live sliders, reset | Section 20 |\n918\t| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits, may cheat | Section 20 |\n919\t| Narrative tone | Section 14 |\n920\t| 10-foot controller-first, DualSense, native keyboard and mouse, glyph switching, Xbox option, no dense screens | Section 15 |\n921\t| Touch-only notice, settings button, attract runs | Section 15 |\n922\t| Done: no TODOs, placeholders, stubs | Sections 23, 24 |\n923\t| Source Information, feedback in chat and About; exit interview with AI Hub block | Sections 20, 24 |\n924\t| Testing, Engine, Prompt Feedback questions | Sections 20, 24: answer them from your real experience |\n925\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7fc0c99-6050-4a36-bbad-13de512b63e9","uuid":"88c74337-1215-4064-abae-078721be0c4d","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:18:31.844Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171824__x10629c/work/PLAN.md","content":"src/systems/          tools, fishing, bugs, dig, trees, flowers, stars, snow, economy, cheese, shop,\n                      museum, home, placement, rating, mail, favours, memory, town rating,\n                      salon, couture, magda, theatre, auction, bureau, cafe, fortune, bus,\n                      festivals/ (seedToss, lanternFloat, soup, longNight), contests\nsrc/ui/               font, glyphs, panel, focus, prompts, keyboard, dialogue, screens/*\nsrc/audio/            voice, composer, songs/*, sfx, ambience\nsrc/data/             items, fish, bugs, fossils, art, furniture, clothes, neighbours, dialogue/*, festivals, year\nsrc/art/              critter, models, textures (everything exported for check.mjs --sheets)\nsrc/net/              transport, hub, broadcast, rtc, host, guest, arena, visit, protocol\nsrc/dev/              tuning, devmode, attract\ntests/                run.mjs, sim/*, play/*, browser/*, hub/\ntools/                build.mjs\n```\n\n- **State:** one plain JSON `world` object is the save. Systems are functions over `world` plus `ctx`; presentation reads it. An event bus (`caught`, `sold`, `gift`, `talked`, `day`) feeds memory, stats and tests.\n- **Data-driven:** adding a fish, neighbour, furniture set, festival, venue or line needs no code, only a data entry and possibly a model def, so the rest of the game can be built later with normal designer tools. Map rows, dialogue, items, songs, tuning and the year table are all plain data; dev mode's Events page lists them automatically.\n- **UI:** a screen stack of plain objects (`open`, `input`, `draw`); focus and hit areas are real data, hover and click resolve against them; `ui.model()` returns the current screen, focus and prompts for tests.\n- **Test handle:** `window.__GAME__` (installed in the `ready` hook) exposes `world`, `ui.model()`, `tuning`, and actions; `status()` gives day, hour, place, beans, debt.\n- **Replays:** `fingerprint()` hashes day, minute, position, beans, debt and collection counts.\n\n---\n\n## 22. Testing\n\n**One command:** `node tests/run.mjs` prints only failures and a one-line count. `--only sim|play|browser|hub|visit|sheets` for one suite. Rerun a suite only after changing what it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Keep one or two browsers open at once and close them; keep all files in your folder; stop only your own PIDs.\n\n| Suite | Runs | Checks |\n|---|---|---|\n| **sim** | Node, under 10 s | Calendar both clocks, Real Clock mapping, weather table, drop tables, cheese patterns, flower rules, debt math, save round trip and migration, map flood fill, line tags resolve, every `Song` parses, every clip validates, voice syllables render and peak under 1 |\n| **play** | `Engine.headless`, under 3 minutes | **Scripted playthrough of the whole game** (below), then replay and compare `fingerprint()` |\n| **browser** | Chromium on `file://` the built HTML, with the flags from section 2 (not TASK.md's shorter list) | Boots with no errors; engine text unchanged in the page; fresh profile `audio.volume.music === 0.1`, and after Settings Reset; stub pad drives title → play → pause; keyboard and mouse the same; prompts switch glyphs with last device, Xbox option works; attract starts at 10 s, a key, a button, a mouse move each exit it, player save untouched; analyser on `audio.master` shows music and voice levels above silence (use `page.keyboard.press` first to unlock); dev mode opens from pause, a slider changes behaviour, Reset All restores; About has every field; Chromium phone and tablet profiles: no touch UI, notice and ⚙ showing, tap opens settings, attract runs; WebKit: friendly no-WebGPU message and no uncaught error |\n| **hub** | Harness (below) | `window.__hubCheck.pass` in every scenario, each run asserted |\n| **visit** | Two pages in one context | BroadcastChannel join and movement seen on both; WebRTC with `iceServers: []` join, invite codes round trip; after leaving, the guest keeps pockets and beans and the host's save is unchanged |\n| **sheets** | `node check.mjs --sheets src/art/models.js` from the kit | Exit 0 |\n\n**Hub harness.** `tools/build.mjs` copies the HTML to `tests/hub/game.html` beside unmodified `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`. Serve that folder with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` and read the port from its first line (`-u`, or it never prints through a pipe). Open `harness.html?game=game.html&auto=1&` plus each of:\n\n- `players=2`\n- `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`\n- `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&scenario=host-leave`\n- `players=2&scenario=server`\n\nWait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`. Confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop the server by its PID in a `finally`.\n\n**Playthrough bot** (`tests/play/`): helpers `walkTo`, `talk`, `pressUI`, `useTool`, `sleepTo`, `nextDay`, driving `input.setKey`/`setPointer` on virtual codes through real input, `engine.step` in batches. Raise game speed for dull stretches. Milestones to assert:\n\n1. New Arrival through bus: name, town, look set; debt 8,000.\n2. Day One errands done; each tool gained; a letter sent; reply next morning.\n3. Fish, bug, fossil caught; a fish sold; beans and memory updated; a neighbour line references it.\n4. Tree shaken, sapling planted, flower watered, hybrid formed, weed pulled, balloon shot, wasp sting and cure, bag buried and beanstalk tripled, shooting-star wish delivered.\n5. Museum donation appears in each wing; Almanac updates.\n6. Each debt tier paid; home tier changes; placement, wallpaper, lamp, stereo, mirror, TV, Bureau grade by mail.\n7. City: every venue entered, one interaction each (cut, plume check, forgery spotted, emote learned, auction won, consignment mailed, critique, coffee buff, fortune).\n8. Cheese bought, sold at profit, goes bad.\n9. Chime composed, heard on the hour; Vesper's record plays on the stereo; pattern drawn, worn, and in the firework.\n10. All four festivals and three contests complete with results. Rocco leaves, Fitch arrives, Rocco returns.\n11. Bed options, Hibernate jump, season reskins, absence effects (faked timestamp), Inspector Pimm on unclean exit.\n12. Long Night, gold Bell at high rating, credits, Year Two starts.\n\n**Stand-ins** are as TASK.md lists: stub `navigator.getGamepads` in `add_init_script`, Playwright touch profiles, Web Audio analysers, two pages. `e.step()` draws nothing: for a picture, set `e.manual = false` and let a frame draw. The software GPU runs 10–20 fps at 1080p, so drive logic in manual time and don't rely on wall-clock speed. Judge the 10-foot view from 1920×1080 screenshots.\n\n**Look at screenshots** for: title, dialogue, Pockets, each season, night, interior, City night, one festival. Check that UI text is legible and actors stand out.\n\n---\n\n## 23. Build order and cut rules\n\nEach step leaves something playable.\n\n1. **Scaffold and packaging.** Title chosen. `tools/build.mjs`, single HTML boots on `file://` with a test scene, test runner printing a count.\n2. **UI kit and input.** Font, panels, focus, glyphs, last-device, virtual pad codes, screen stack, keyboard, settings shell, music 10%.\n3. **Village walk.** Map data, terrain, season surfaces, collision, camera, turns, occluders, wind, one cottage, your avatar and rig.\n4. **Neighbours talk.** Critter generator, clips, routines, dialogue, voices. By now it should be charming. If it isn't, fix it before anything else.\n5. **Verbs.** Tools, fishing, bugs, digging, trees, flowers, reveal, puns, Pockets.\n6. **Time.** Clocks, calendar, weather, seasons, day and night, bed, absence, hourly music.\n7. **Economy and home.** Store, debt, Bean Jar, placement, interactive furniture, Day One script, mail.\n8. **Town services.** Museum, Hall, Mothwick's, patterns, Chime, town rating, Almanac, map.\n9. **The City.** Bus and ballads, boulevard, crowd, neon, all venues.\n10. **The year.** Festivals, contests, Vesper, cheese, Rocco's story, ending, Inspector Pimm.\n11. **Multiplayer.** Hub round and harness suite first, then Visit.\n12. **Shell.** Attract, dev mode, About, final title flow, touch-only.\n13. **Full playthrough suite, polish, content to target.** Then the finish checklist.\n\n**If effort runs short:** cut content to floors first. Next, simplify a venue's fun to a smaller interaction, not to a bare menu. **Never cut:** Day One, neighbours and memory, City, the four festivals, the 10% music rule, controller/keyboard/mouse parity, Settings, attract, dev mode with sliders, Hub pass, About, exit interview. If one Visit transport can't be finished, remove its menu entry entirely: TASK.md allows no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. Anything unfinished must not be visible.\n\n---\n\n## 24. Finish checklist and TASK.md coverage\n\n**Before you finish:**\n\n- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs` shows zero failures; output of the last full run is real, not remembered.\n- [ ] Single HTML in the place TASK.md names, opens by double-click on `file://`, no network needed. No TODOs, placeholders or stubs anywhere (`grep`).\n- [ ] About/Credits filled with Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, the same text as your chat reply. \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Usage says \"added on import\".\n- [ ] Exit interview file written in exactly TASK.md's shape, parses as JSON. Suggested `aiHub` values: type `Game`, genre `Life Sim`, source `Animal Crossing: City Folk`, series `Animal Crossing`, players `1–4`, tags from `pixel art`, `3d`, `comedy`, `exploration`, `versus`, plus your name, tagline, description and controls line.\n- [ ] Every process you started is stopped; no stray files outside your folder.\n\n**Where this plan answers each line of TASK.md:**\n\n| TASK.md | Here |\n|---|---|\n| Source | Header, section 1 |\n| Genre, Perspective | Section 1 |\n| Game Title (riff, 50 ideas, real RNG) | Sections 0, 1; left to the builder |\n| Research | Done for this plan; further research is your choice |\n| Package: single HTML, dependencies | Section 2 |\n| Tech Stack: riftlight kit, engine as ships | Section 2 |\n| Art Direction | Section 16 |\n| Animation | Section 17 |\n| Audio: code only, gibberish voices, music 10% | Section 18 |\n| Online; Multiplayer: Hub and on its own | Section 19 |\n| Working environment and deliverable, shared machine | Sections 0, 2, 22 |\n| This computer, its stand-ins, sharing it | Sections 2, 22 (and one correction: browser flags, section 2) |\n| Performance out of scope, unlimited budget | Sections 16, 22 |\n| Testing: headless, one command, playthrough, no debate subagents | Section 22 |\n| Effort | Your call; section 23 orders it |\n| Goal and scope: original, feature-complete vertical slice, designer-extensible | Sections 1, 12, 21 |\n| Dev mode from pause, live sliders, reset | Section 20 |\n| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits, may cheat | Section 20 |\n| Narrative tone | Section 14 |\n| 10-foot controller-first, DualSense, native keyboard and mouse, glyph switching, Xbox option, no dense screens | Section 15 |\n| Touch-only notice, settings button, attract runs | Section 15 |\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders, stubs | Sections 23, 24 |\n| Source Information, feedback in chat and About; exit interview with AI Hub block | Sections 20, 24 |\n| Testing, Engine, Prompt Feedback questions | Sections 20, 24: answer them from your real experience |\n","numLines":126,"startLine":800,"totalLines":925}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017FnreoeENSpqcZwiSGFnBg","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
