t-mode deadlock and the pause-menu reopen loop. The rest were wrong-feeling rather than game-stopping.' },\n    { q: 'What was the most helpful test you ran?', a: 'The scripted playthrough in Node on Engine.headless. It drives the real game objects and real input through New Arrival, Day One errands, tools, shop, museum, home, every City venue, cheese, Vesper, all four festivals, three contests, Rocco leaving, bed, seasons, absence, Pimm and the Long Night ending, in about a minute, and printed exactly which step broke.' },\n    { q: 'What was the least helpful test you ran?', a: 'The WebKit no-WebGPU check and the sim-suite counts of dialogue lines: both pass trivially and never failed for a reason that mattered. The sheets check does not apply because the art is built from primitives in code rather than ModelDefs, so it was not run.' },\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    { q: 'What did you like most about the engine?', a: 'That everything shares one look. Toon bands, pixel outlines and a 16-colour palette make code-built boxes and spheres read as intentional art, and the same game runs in Node headless, so a whole playthrough is testable without a GPU.' },\n    { q: 'Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', a: 'Yes: ctx.random.fork() returns an object, not a function; NavGrid origin is the corner of cell (0,0); manual engine.step() loops starve promises unless you yield between steps; Precipitation needs intensity set to 0 as well as visible=false; and the Euler signs on a custom rig (negative X swings a limb forward) took a few renders to confirm.' },\n    { q: 'What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?', a: 'The whole interface layer: a proportional bitmap font, panels, focus and pointer hit-testing, glyph prompts that follow the last device, dialogue with a typewriter and voice, screens for pockets, map, almanac, keyboard and dev mode, all on the 480x270 HUD. After that, the critter generator and the data-driven rooms.' },\n    { q: 'What was the hardest part about using the engine?', a: 'Mapping the pointer (normalised device coordinates) onto HUD pixels under the fixed-aspect letterbox, and drawing a lot of mixed-case text cheaply with hud.sprite instead of hud.text, which only has capitals.' },\n    { q: 'What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', a: 'None. pixel-engine.js is byte-identical to the kit, and a test asserts its text appears unchanged in the page.' },\n    { q: 'If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?', a: 'An awaitable step helper (engine.stepAsync(n)) that yields between frames so scripted games with async scenes can be tested without hand-written microtask flushes, plus a callable form of ctx.random.' },\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    { q: 'What did you like most about this prompt?', a: 'The verified recipes. The browser-flags correction (the engine needs --use-angle=swiftshader), the pause rule and the music-volume arithmetic each saved real debugging time, and the pillars gave every cut a reason.' },\n    { q: 'What was confusing?', a: 'How to square \"feature-complete, nothing stubbed\" with a plan that is far larger than one session: the cut rules say what never to cut but not how large each system should be. Also whether the deliverable name keeps the spaces of the title and source (I removed them).' },\n    { q: 'What was the hardest thing to do?', a: 'Choosing scope. Eight venues, four festivals, three contests, a shared-world round, attract mode, dev mode and a year of dialogue each deserve a week; finding a version of each that is real, hands-on and small was the hard part.' },\n    { q: 'What would you change in this prompt?', a: 'Give each section a size budget and a ranked minimal slice, say which cross-checks matter most (hub harness, 10% music, attract timing), and state explicitly whether Visit may be dropped when only one transport is built.' },\n  ],\n};\nE\npython3 - <<'E'\np='src/data/about.js'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"// About / Credits content. Mirrors the final chat reply and exit-interview.json.\",\"// About / Credits content. Mirrors the final chat reply and exit-interview.json.\\nimport { FEEDBACK } from './feedback.js';\")\ns=s.replace(\"  feedback: { testing: [], engine: [], prompt: [] },\",\"  feedback: FEEDBACK,\")\ns=s.replace(\"['Online', 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1 shared-world round (\\\"The Bean Bazaar\\\"), own card, harness checked; Visit via BroadcastChannel']\",\"['Online', 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1 shared-world round (\\\"The Bean Bazaar\\\") with its own card, passing the hub harness; no other network features']\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n \"Online\" src/data/about.js | head -2; mkdir -p tools; cat > tools/exit-interview.mjs <<'E'\n// Writes exit-interview.json in the work root, in exactly TASK.md's shape, from src/data/about.js + feedback.js.\nimport { writeFileSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'; import { resolve, dirname } from 'node:path'; import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\nimport { ABOUT } from '../src/data/about.js'; import { FEEDBACK } from '../src/data/feedback.js';\nconst root = resolve(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '..', '..'); const get = (k) => ABOUT.source.find(([a]) => a === k)[1];\nconst out = {\n  sourceInformation: { date: get('Date'), source: get('Source'), plan: 'PLAN.md', genre: 'Life sim / social sim (AI Hub genre: Life Sim)', perspective: 'Isometric 3/4 orthographic diorama camera (engine iso preset, yaw 45, pitch 35) with 90-degree camera turns; same camera indoors as a dollhouse cutaway', tech: get('Tech'), artDirection: get('Art Direction'), animation: get('Animation'), audio: get('Audio'), online: get('Online'), testing: get('Testing'), research: get('Research'), environment: get('Environment'), model: get('Model'), usage: 'added on import' },\n  testingFeedback: FEEDBACK.testing.map(x => x.a), engineFeedback: FEEDBACK.engine.map(x => x.a), promptFeedback: FEEDBACK.prompt.map(x => x.a),\n  aiHub: { name: 'Creditor Creek', tagline: 'A village, a goose, a very large bill', description: 'Step off the last bus and owe a goose 8,000 beans: catch, dig, sell, befriend eight odd neighbours through a 16-day year, and ride the bus to a neon City of salons, auctions and a magpie. Also a five-minute shared-world fair for up to four.', type: 'Game', genre: 'Life Sim', source: 'Animal Crossing: City Folk', series: 'Animal Crossing', players: '1\\u20134', controls: 'Move: stick or WASD. Talk and open: Cross or E. Tool: Square or Space. Pockets: Triangle or Tab. Run: hold Circle or Shift. Pause: Options or Esc.', tags: ['pixel art', '3d', 'comedy', 'exploration', 'versus', 'story'] },\n};\nwriteFileSync(resolve(root, 'exit-interview.json'), JSON.stringify(out, null, 2)); JSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve(root, 'exit-interview.json'), 'utf8')); console.log('exit-interview.json written');\nE\nnode tools/exit-interview.mjs"}}}
