{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgkob2tup9anjaUcBku3h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"WebRTC invite codes for Visit are built, and the full test run on the final build passes with 426 checks, 0 failures.\n\n- **Visit by code:** the Visit menu now has \"Open my gate by code\" and \"Visit by code\", on top of the two-windows-on-one-computer option. The host makes a Gate Pass, the guest pastes it and sends back a Reply Slip, and the host pastes that to connect. Codes come out around a thousand characters, shown in a pixel-styled box that says it needs a keyboard and has a Copy button. The test runs the whole exchange between two separate browser sessions with no ICE servers. The guest arrives in the host's town and the host sees them move. I did not test a real connection across two different computers or a connection through a public STUN server.\n- **Art in the engine's model format:** five props are now ModelDefs actually used in the game: the bell, notice board, bench, market stall and cheese wheel. The kit's sheets check passes on all five with no problems or warnings. The rest of the art (characters, buildings, trees, interiors) is still built from primitives in code.\n- **Deliverables:** the About screen, `exit-interview.json` and the built HTML now describe Visit with both transports and say how many props are ModelDefs.\n\n**Still open from PLAN.md:** the plan's animals and buildings as ModelDefs, and the full art list in section 16 (own tree stages, fish and bug models per species, etc.). I built simplified versions of many of those."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":981,"cache_read_input_tokens":614972,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":981},"output_tokens":482,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_tr