{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkpvoXRJhTX1fZvQXdsj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I checked the remaining species and Magda's forgery mark by screenshot, and made wombat and quokka look different. The sim, play and browser suites pass on this build (275, 92 and 41 checks, 0 failures); I did not re-run the AI Hub harness, Visit or sheets suites after the art change, which only touched critter shapes.\n\n- **Silhouettes:** with this round's screenshots every species has been seen at least once. The ones that read most clearly are the goat (horns and beard), llama (long ears and neck), dormouse (big ears) and hippo, which join the earlier moose, platypus, lemur, yak, goose, capybara, snail, pangolin and moth. A couple of the 14 I checked this round were partly hidden behind the market stall, so the check is rough.\n- **Wombat and quokka:** the wombat is now larger and stockier and the quokka smaller with a taller head, so in the screenshot they're no longer the same blob.\n- **Forgery mark:** the screenshot shows a forged painting in Magda's shop with the red mark in the lower-right corner of the frame.\n\n**Still open:**\n- **Visit across two computers:** the Gate Pass and Reply Slip exchange has only been tested between separate browser sessions on this machine. I have no second computer here to try a real LAN or internet connection.\n- **Flamingo and badger:** in my screenshot they looked similar because I coloured them both orange; in the game the flamingo is red.\n\nThe built HTML, `exit-interview.json`, the dev folder and `PROGRESS.md` are all in the work folder. The About and feedback text is unchanged."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":387,"cache_read_input_tokens":696178,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":387},"output_tokens":532,"service