{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkqERcZP8DnPT1gMyYK3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I redid the 27-species check against an empty beach, in the game's real colours with no stall or trees behind them. Every species reads as distinct in those screenshots. The wombat and quokka I judged from the earlier shots against the beach, because they sat at the screen edge in the clean ones.\n\n- **Clearest silhouettes:** the moose has antlers with laundry, the platypus a bill, the armadillo banded back plates, the lemur a ringed tail, the yak a shaggy bulk, the goose a long neck and orange bill, the snail a red shell, the flamingo very long legs and neck, the magpie a black-and-white body with a blue tail, the goat horns and the hermit a cabinet on its back.\n- **Closest pairs:** the stoat and ferret are the nearest to each other (both small, sand/orange, long-bodied). They are told apart by a tail tip and ear shape, which at game size is subtle.\n\nI can't do the real two-computer Visit test: there is no second machine on this computer. The invite-code exchange passes between separate browser sessions here, and I have never run it across a LAN or the internet.\n\nI did not change any code this round, so the last full suite run (426 checks, 0 failures) still applies. The built HTML, `exit-interview.json` (which matches the feedback text in the game) and `PROGRESS.md` are in the work folder."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1367,"cache_read_input_tokens":710931,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1367},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7fc0c99-6050-4a36-bbad-13de512b63e9","uuid":"4dd33f19-06da